' =============================================================================
' BOMBSQUAD - a minesweeping game in the style of the Windows classic
' =============================================================================
'
' Written in DBasic, drawn with Ebitengine.
'
' WHAT THIS PROGRAM IS
'   BombSquad plays the game that shipped with Windows for twenty years: a
'   grid of covered squares, some of which hide mines. Uncover every square
'   that is NOT a mine and you win. Uncover a mine and the game is over.
'
'   (The Windows one is called Minesweeper. That name is Microsoft's, so
'   ours is BombSquad -- same game, different badge.)
'
'   Everything the original did, this does:
'     * Beginner / Intermediate / Expert / Custom board sizes
'     * A mine counter and a seconds timer, both as red 7-segment LEDs
'     * The smiley-face reset button (it goes "ooh" while you click, wears
'       sunglasses when you win, and dies when you lose)
'     * Left click uncovers, right click flags, right click again marks "?"
'     * "Chording": press BOTH buttons on an uncovered number and, if you
'       have flagged exactly that many neighbours, all the other neighbours
'       are uncovered at once
'     * The first square you click is never a mine
'     * A real drop-down menu bar with Game and Help menus
'     * Permanent high-score tables, one per difficulty, saved to disk
'     * Sound effects (switchable from the Game menu)
'
' HOW THE PROGRAM IS ORGANISED (read in this order)
'   1. CONSTANTS      - sizes, colours and "magic numbers" given names
'   2. TYPES          - the shapes of our data (the Game, a menu item, ...)
'   3. SPRITES        - every picture in the game, drawn once at start-up
'   4. SOUND          - square-wave beeps generated as raw audio samples
'   5. HIGH SCORES    - loading and saving the score file
'   6. BOARD LOGIC    - laying mines, uncovering squares, winning, losing
'   7. INPUT          - mouse, keyboard, menus and dialog boxes
'   8. DRAWING        - painting the board, the header and the dialogs
'   9. MAIN           - starts everything up
'
' HOW A GAME PROGRAM WORKS (the big picture)
'   Ebitengine calls two of our routines sixty times a second:
'     Update()  - look at the mouse and keyboard, change the game state
'     Draw()    - paint the current game state onto the screen
'   Nothing else happens in between. That is the whole game loop.
'
' CONTROLS
'   Left click .............. uncover a square
'   Right click ............. flag / question-mark / clear a square
'   Both buttons (or middle)  chord: uncover the neighbours of a number
'   Click the smiley ........ start a new game
'   F2 ...................... start a new game
'   F1 ...................... help
'   Esc ..................... close a menu or a dialog box
' =============================================================================


' =============================================================================
' A FEW WORDS ABOUT THE CODE ITSELF, IF YOU ARE NEW TO THIS
' =============================================================================
'
' Everything below is built out of about eight ideas. Once you know these,
' the whole program reads like English.
'
'   DIM x AS INTEGER
'       Make a box called x that can hold a whole number. "DIM" is just
'       BASIC's way of saying "I would like a box, please." A box always has
'       a type, which is the kind of thing it is allowed to hold: INTEGER for
'       whole numbers, STRING for text, BOOLEAN for yes/no.
'
'   CONST CELL AS INTEGER = 16
'       The same thing, except the value can never change. We use these for
'       numbers that would otherwise be mysterious. "CELL" tells you what 16
'       means; a bare 16 sprinkled through the code tells you nothing.
'
'   DIM xs AS []INTEGER
'       A LIST of whole numbers, as long as we like. xs[0] is the first one
'       (computers start counting at nought, not one). Len(xs) is how many
'       there are. APPEND(xs, 5) puts another one on the end.
'
'   TYPE Game ... END TYPE
'       A shape for our own kind of box, made by gluing several boxes
'       together. Once we have said what a Game looks like, "DIM g AS Game"
'       makes one, and g.Rows reaches inside it for the part called Rows.
'
'   POINTER TO Game
'       Not the game itself, but a note saying WHERE the game is - like a
'       house number instead of the house. This matters: if you hand someone
'       a copy of the house they can redecorate it and you will never see the
'       change, but if you hand them the address they are working on the real
'       thing. Routines that need to CHANGE the game always take a pointer.
'
'   SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) Reveal(at AS INTEGER)
'       A routine that belongs to the Game, so it is written as g.Reveal(5)
'       rather than Reveal(g, 5). Read the bit in brackets at the front as
'       "given a game, called g, ...". A SUB does something; a FUNCTION does
'       something AND hands an answer back.
'
'   MAP OF STRING TO POINTER TO ebiten.Image
'       A lookup table. You put things in under a label and fetch them back
'       out by the same label, like a coat check.
'
'   IMPORT "..." AS name
'       Borrow somebody else's code and call it "name" from now on.
'
' One last thing you will see everywhere: Sng(x). Ebitengine's drawing
' routines want their numbers in a slightly different flavour from the whole
' numbers we count squares in, and Sng() converts between the two. It does
' not change the value, only the flavour.
'
' =============================================================================


' ======================== IMPORTS ========================
' These pull in code other people wrote so we do not have to write it.

' Ebitengine: opens the window, runs the game loop, draws images.
IMPORT "github.com/hajimehoshi/ebiten/v2" AS ebiten

' vector: draws filled rectangles, circles and lines.
IMPORT "github.com/hajimehoshi/ebiten/v2/vector" AS vector

' ebitenutil: a quick way to draw text.
IMPORT "github.com/hajimehoshi/ebiten/v2/ebitenutil" AS ebitenutil

' inpututil: tells us the moment a key or button is pressed or released,
' as opposed to whether it is being held down right now.
IMPORT "github.com/hajimehoshi/ebiten/v2/inpututil" AS inpututil

' audio: plays our sound effects.
IMPORT "github.com/hajimehoshi/ebiten/v2/audio" AS audio

' color: lets us say "this shade of grey" as a number of red, green and blue.
IMPORT "image/color" AS color

' strings: strings.NewReader turns generated sound data into something the
' audio system can read.
IMPORT "strings" AS strings

' strconv: turns text like "16" into the number 16.
IMPORT "strconv" AS strconv

' fmt: prints messages to the terminal if something goes wrong.
IMPORT "fmt" AS fmt

' os and filepath: used to find the player's home folder so the high score
' table can be kept somewhere permanent.
IMPORT "os" AS os
IMPORT "path/filepath" AS filepath

' time: used to date-stamp a high score.
IMPORT "time" AS time


' ======================== 1. CONSTANTS ========================

' --- Sizes, in pixels ---

' One square of the minefield is 16x16 pixels, exactly like the original.
CONST CELL AS INTEGER = 16

' The chiselled 3D frames around the header and the minefield are 3px thick.
CONST FRAME AS INTEGER = 3

' The plain grey breathing space between those frames.
CONST GAP AS INTEGER = 6

' The raised border drawn around the whole window.
CONST OUTER AS INTEGER = 3

' Height of the menu bar strip along the top.
CONST MENUH AS INTEGER = 20

' Height of the header panel that holds the counter, face and timer.
CONST HDRH AS INTEGER = 33

' A single LED digit is 13 wide and 23 tall; three of them make a display.
CONST LEDW AS INTEGER = 13
CONST LEDH AS INTEGER = 23

' The smiley-face button is a 24x24 square.
CONST FACEW AS INTEGER = 24

' Every character of our text font is 6 pixels wide and 16 tall.
CONST CHW AS INTEGER = 6
CONST CHH AS INTEGER = 16

' --- What is in a square (the "state" of a cell) ---
CONST ST_COVERED AS INTEGER = 0    ' untouched
CONST ST_REVEALED AS INTEGER = 1   ' uncovered
CONST ST_FLAG AS INTEGER = 2       ' player planted a flag here
CONST ST_QUERY AS INTEGER = 3      ' player marked it with a question mark

' --- The four difficulty levels ---
CONST LV_BEGINNER AS INTEGER = 0
CONST LV_INTER AS INTEGER = 1
CONST LV_EXPERT AS INTEGER = 2
CONST LV_CUSTOM AS INTEGER = 3

' --- Which face the smiley button is wearing ---
CONST FACE_SMILE AS INTEGER = 0    ' just playing
CONST FACE_OOH AS INTEGER = 1      ' the player is holding the mouse down
CONST FACE_WIN AS INTEGER = 2      ' sunglasses
CONST FACE_DEAD AS INTEGER = 3     ' X eyes

' --- Which dialog box (if any) is on screen ---
CONST DLG_NONE AS INTEGER = 0
CONST DLG_CUSTOM AS INTEGER = 1    ' "Custom Field" - pick your own size
CONST DLG_TIMES AS INTEGER = 2     ' the high score tables
CONST DLG_NAME AS INTEGER = 3      ' "you got a high score, type your name"
CONST DLG_ABOUT AS INTEGER = 4
CONST DLG_HELP AS INTEGER = 5

' --- Menu command numbers ---
' Every clickable menu line has a number so that one routine can carry out
' whatever the player picked.
CONST MI_NONE AS INTEGER = 0
CONST MI_NEW AS INTEGER = 1
CONST MI_BEG AS INTEGER = 2
CONST MI_INT AS INTEGER = 3
CONST MI_EXP AS INTEGER = 4
CONST MI_CUSTOM AS INTEGER = 5
CONST MI_MARKS AS INTEGER = 6
CONST MI_COLOR AS INTEGER = 7
CONST MI_SOUND AS INTEGER = 8
CONST MI_TIMES AS INTEGER = 9
CONST MI_EXIT AS INTEGER = 10
CONST MI_HELP AS INTEGER = 11
CONST MI_ABOUT AS INTEGER = 12

' --- Limits for a custom board, copied from the original game ---
CONST MIN_ROWS AS INTEGER = 9
CONST MAX_ROWS AS INTEGER = 24
CONST MIN_COLS AS INTEGER = 9
CONST MAX_COLS AS INTEGER = 30
CONST MIN_MINES AS INTEGER = 10

' How many names we keep in each high score table.
CONST TOP_N AS INTEGER = 5

' How much of a name the Best Times table shows, in characters, and how wide
' that makes one column. A column holds "N. " (3 characters), the name, a
' small gap, and the time right-aligned at the far edge (up to 3 digits).
CONST NAME_CHARS AS INTEGER = 12
CONST SCORE_COLW AS INTEGER = (3 + NAME_CHARS + 1 + 3) * CHW
CONST SCORE_COLGAP AS INTEGER = SCORE_COLW + 8

' The timer stops counting here, just like the original three-digit display.
CONST MAX_SECS AS INTEGER = 999


' ======================== 2. TYPES ========================

' One line of a drop-down menu.
TYPE MenuItem
    DIM Label AS STRING      ' the words shown, e.g. "Beginner"
    DIM Accel AS STRING      ' shortcut text shown on the right, e.g. "F2"
    DIM Id AS INTEGER        ' which MI_* command this line runs
    DIM Sep AS BOOLEAN       ' TRUE if this is just a dividing line
    DIM Checked AS BOOLEAN   ' TRUE to draw a tick next to it
END TYPE

' One line of a high score table.
TYPE ScoreEntry
    DIM Secs AS INTEGER      ' how many seconds the winning game took
    DIM Who AS STRING        ' the player's name
    DIM DateStr AS STRING    ' the day it happened, as YYYY-MM-DD
END TYPE

' Everything the game needs to know, all in one place. Ebitengine calls
' Update() and Draw() on a pointer to this, so any routine that gets
' "g AS POINTER TO Game" can see and change the whole game.
TYPE Game
    ' --- the minefield itself ---
    DIM Rows AS INTEGER          ' how many rows of squares
    DIM Cols AS INTEGER          ' how many columns of squares
    DIM NMines AS INTEGER        ' how many mines are buried
    DIM Level AS INTEGER         ' LV_BEGINNER .. LV_CUSTOM

    ' Three lists, one entry per square, laid out row by row. The square at
    ' row r and column c lives at position r * Cols + c in each list.
    DIM Mine AS []BOOLEAN        ' TRUE if a mine is buried here
    DIM St AS []INTEGER          ' ST_COVERED / ST_REVEALED / ST_FLAG / ST_QUERY
    DIM Adj AS []INTEGER         ' how many of the 8 neighbours are mines

    ' --- how the game is going ---
    DIM Started AS BOOLEAN       ' TRUE once the first square has been clicked
    DIM Over AS BOOLEAN          ' TRUE when the game has finished
    DIM Won AS BOOLEAN           ' TRUE if it finished by winning
    DIM BoomIdx AS INTEGER       ' which square blew up (-1 if none)
    DIM NShown AS INTEGER        ' how many squares are uncovered so far
    DIM NFlags AS INTEGER        ' how many flags are planted
    DIM Secs AS INTEGER          ' the timer, in seconds
    DIM Ticks AS INTEGER         ' counts frames so we know when a second passed

    ' --- options from the Game menu ---
    DIM UseMarks AS BOOLEAN      ' allow the "?" mark
    DIM UseColor AS BOOLEAN      ' colour numbers, or plain black and white
    DIM UseSound AS BOOLEAN      ' play sound effects

    ' --- where things are on screen (worked out by Relayout) ---
    DIM ScrW AS INTEGER          ' width the board itself needs, in pixels
    DIM ScrH AS INTEGER          ' height the board itself needs
    DIM ViewW AS INTEGER         ' width of the window (bigger if a dialog needs it)
    DIM ViewH AS INTEGER         ' height of the window
    DIM OX AS INTEGER            ' where the board sits if the window is bigger
    DIM OY AS INTEGER
    DIM GX AS INTEGER            ' left edge of the minefield
    DIM GY AS INTEGER            ' top edge of the minefield
    DIM HdrX AS INTEGER          ' left edge of the header panel
    DIM HdrY AS INTEGER          ' top edge of the header panel
    DIM Zoom AS INTEGER          ' how many real pixels per game pixel

    ' --- the mouse ---
    DIM MX AS INTEGER            ' cursor position, in game pixels
    DIM MY AS INTEGER
    DIM PressIdx AS INTEGER      ' square currently held down (-1 for none)
    DIM Chording AS BOOLEAN      ' TRUE while both buttons are held
    DIM Swallow AS BOOLEAN       ' ignore the second button coming back up
    DIM FaceDown AS BOOLEAN      ' TRUE while the smiley button is held
    DIM Pressing AS BOOLEAN      ' TRUE while a button is held over the field
    DIM Face AS INTEGER          ' FACE_SMILE / FACE_OOH / FACE_WIN / FACE_DEAD
    DIM Quit AS BOOLEAN          ' set by Game/Exit to close the window

    ' --- the menu bar ---
    DIM MenuOpen AS INTEGER      ' -1 closed, 0 = Game menu, 1 = Help menu
    DIM MenuHot AS INTEGER       ' which line the mouse is over (-1 for none)
    DIM MenuItems AS []MenuItem  ' the lines of the menu that is open
    DIM MenuX AS INTEGER         ' where the drop-down is drawn
    DIM MenuW AS INTEGER

    ' --- dialog boxes ---
    DIM Dlg AS INTEGER           ' DLG_NONE when no dialog is up
    DIM Focus AS INTEGER         ' which text box has the cursor
    DIM FldRows AS STRING        ' what is typed in the Custom Field boxes
    DIM FldCols AS STRING
    DIM FldMines AS STRING
    DIM NameBuf AS STRING        ' what is typed in the high score name box
    DIM NameLvl AS INTEGER       ' which table that high score belongs to
    DIM NameRank AS INTEGER      ' the position it earned
    DIM Blink AS INTEGER         ' counts frames so the text cursor blinks

    ' --- high scores, one table per standard difficulty ---
    DIM ScBeg AS []ScoreEntry
    DIM ScInt AS []ScoreEntry
    DIM ScExp AS []ScoreEntry
    DIM ScFile AS STRING         ' full path of the file they are saved in

    ' --- pictures, all drawn once when the game starts ---
    DIM ImgCovered AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image   ' an untouched square
    DIM ImgBlank AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image     ' an uncovered empty square
    DIM ImgNum AS []POINTER TO ebiten.Image     ' the numbers 1 to 8
    DIM ImgFlag AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image
    DIM ImgQuery AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image     ' "?" on a covered square
    DIM ImgQueryDn AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image   ' "?" on an uncovered square
    DIM ImgMine AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image
    DIM ImgBoom AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image      ' the mine you stepped on
    DIM ImgWrong AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image     ' a flag that was wrong
    DIM ImgLed AS []POINTER TO ebiten.Image     ' LED digits 0-9 and a minus
    DIM ImgFace AS []POINTER TO ebiten.Image    ' the four smiley faces
    DIM ImgFaceDn AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image    ' the smiley button pushed in

    ' --- sound effects ---
    DIM SndTick AS POINTER TO audio.Player      ' one per second
    DIM SndOpen AS POINTER TO audio.Player      ' uncovering squares
    DIM SndFlag AS POINTER TO audio.Player      ' planting a flag
    DIM SndBoom AS POINTER TO audio.Player      ' you lose
    DIM SndWin AS POINTER TO audio.Player       ' you win
END TYPE


' Pictures of text we have already drawn, kept so we never draw the same
' words twice. A map declared out here like this is ready to use straight
' away; one declared inside a TYPE would have to be created by hand first.
DIM GTxt AS MAP OF STRING TO POINTER TO ebiten.Image

' The one and only audio context. Ebitengine allows exactly one per program,
' so we make it in Main and keep it here where everything can reach it.
DIM GAudio AS POINTER TO audio.Context


' ======================== 3. SPRITES (all the pictures) ========================
'
' Think of these as rubber stamps. Carving a stamp is slow, but once it is
' carved you can bang out hundreds of copies in no time. So we carve every
' picture the game needs ONCE, when it starts, and after that drawing a
' square is just "stamp picture number 3 here".
'
' An Expert board has 480 squares and gets repainted sixty times a second.
' That is nearly 29,000 squares a second. Carving each one from scratch
' every time would bring the game to its knees; stamping them does not.

' --- The colour palette of Windows 3.1, which is what the original used ---
DIM CGray AS color.RGBA = color.RGBA{R: 192, G: 192, B: 192, A: 255}
DIM CWhite AS color.RGBA = color.RGBA{R: 255, G: 255, B: 255, A: 255}
DIM CDark AS color.RGBA = color.RGBA{R: 128, G: 128, B: 128, A: 255}
DIM CBlack AS color.RGBA = color.RGBA{R: 0, G: 0, B: 0, A: 255}
DIM CRed AS color.RGBA = color.RGBA{R: 255, G: 0, B: 0, A: 255}
DIM CNavy AS color.RGBA = color.RGBA{R: 0, G: 0, B: 128, A: 255}
DIM CYellow AS color.RGBA = color.RGBA{R: 255, G: 255, B: 0, A: 255}

' The eight number colours, in order: 1 is blue, 2 is green, 3 is red and
' so on. These are the exact colours the original game used.
DIM GNumClr AS []color.RGBA = []color.RGBA{ _
    color.RGBA{R: 0, G: 0, B: 255, A: 255}, _
    color.RGBA{R: 0, G: 128, B: 0, A: 255}, _
    color.RGBA{R: 255, G: 0, B: 0, A: 255}, _
    color.RGBA{R: 0, G: 0, B: 128, A: 255}, _
    color.RGBA{R: 128, G: 0, B: 0, A: 255}, _
    color.RGBA{R: 0, G: 128, B: 128, A: 255}, _
    color.RGBA{R: 0, G: 0, B: 0, A: 255}, _
    color.RGBA{R: 128, G: 128, B: 128, A: 255}}


' Rect paints a solid block of colour. Every picture in this game is made
' of nothing but solid blocks, so this is our one and only paintbrush.
SUB Rect(dst AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image, x AS INTEGER, y AS INTEGER, w AS INTEGER, h AS INTEGER, clr AS color.RGBA)
    vector.DrawFilledRect(dst, Sng(x), Sng(y), Sng(w), Sng(h), clr, FALSE)
END SUB


' Bevel draws the chiselled 3D edge that Windows used everywhere.
'
' HOW THE TRICK WORKS
'   Your eyes decide whether something sticks out or goes in by looking at
'   where the light is. Pretend a lamp is shining from the top left of the
'   screen. A button that sticks OUT would catch the light along its top and
'   left edges, and cast a shadow along its bottom and right ones. So: paint
'   those two edges white and the other two dark grey, and a flat rectangle
'   of pixels turns into a button you want to press. Swap the two and the
'   same rectangle looks like a dent.
'
'   raised = TRUE  : light on the top and left  -> sticks OUT
'   raised = FALSE : light on the bottom and right -> pressed IN
SUB Bevel(dst AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image, x AS INTEGER, y AS INTEGER, w AS INTEGER, h AS INTEGER, t AS INTEGER, raised AS BOOLEAN)
    DIM hi AS color.RGBA = CWhite
    DIM lo AS color.RGBA = CDark
    IF NOT raised THEN
        hi = CDark
        lo = CWhite
    ENDIF
    Rect(dst, x, y, w, t, hi)                  ' top edge
    Rect(dst, x, y, t, h, hi)                  ' left edge
    Rect(dst, x, y + h - t, w, t, lo)          ' bottom edge
    Rect(dst, x + w - t, y, t, h, lo)          ' right edge
END SUB


' GlyphRows hands back the little picture of a character, drawn as text.
' A "#" means "paint a pixel here" and a "." means "leave it alone".
' Each glyph is 6 pixels wide and 9 tall - big and bold, like the original.
'   1 to 8  = the neighbour-count digits
'   9       = a question mark
FUNCTION GlyphRows(n AS INTEGER) AS []STRING
    SELECT CASE n
        CASE 1
            RETURN []STRING{"..##..", ".###..", "..##..", "..##..", "..##..", "..##..", "..##..", "..##..", "######"}
        CASE 2
            RETURN []STRING{".####.", "##..##", "....##", "....##", "...##.", "..##..", ".##...", "##....", "######"}
        CASE 3
            RETURN []STRING{".####.", "##..##", "....##", "...##.", "..###.", "....##", "....##", "##..##", ".####."}
        CASE 4
            RETURN []STRING{"...##.", "..###.", ".####.", "##.##.", "##.##.", "######", "...##.", "...##.", "...##."}
        CASE 5
            RETURN []STRING{"######", "##....", "##....", "#####.", "....##", "....##", "....##", "##..##", ".####."}
        CASE 6
            RETURN []STRING{"..###.", ".##...", "##....", "##....", "#####.", "##..##", "##..##", "##..##", ".####."}
        CASE 7
            RETURN []STRING{"######", "....##", "....##", "...##.", "...##.", "..##..", "..##..", ".##...", ".##..."}
        CASE 8
            RETURN []STRING{".####.", "##..##", "##..##", "##..##", ".####.", "##..##", "##..##", "##..##", ".####."}
        CASE 9
            RETURN []STRING{".####.", "##..##", "....##", "...##.", "..##..", "..##..", "......", "..##..", "..##.."}
    END SELECT
    RETURN []STRING{}
END FUNCTION


' DrawGlyph stamps one of those little character pictures onto an image.
SUB DrawGlyph(dst AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image, n AS INTEGER, px AS INTEGER, py AS INTEGER, clr AS color.RGBA)
    DIM rows AS []STRING = GlyphRows(n)
    DIM r AS INTEGER
    DIM c AS INTEGER
    FOR r = 0 TO Len(rows) - 1
        FOR c = 0 TO Len(rows[r]) - 1
            IF Mid(rows[r], c + 1, 1) = "#" THEN
                Rect(dst, px + c, py + r, 1, 1, clr)
            ENDIF
        NEXT
    NEXT
END SUB


' --- The two basic squares ---

' A square nobody has touched: a raised grey button.
FUNCTION MakeCovered() AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image
    DIM img AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image = ebiten.NewImage(CELL, CELL)
    Rect(img, 0, 0, CELL, CELL, CGray)
    Bevel(img, 0, 0, CELL, CELL, 2, TRUE)
    RETURN img
END FUNCTION

' An uncovered square: flat grey with a thin dark line along the top and
' left. Those lines are what make the field look like graph paper.
FUNCTION MakeBlank() AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image
    DIM img AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image = ebiten.NewImage(CELL, CELL)
    Rect(img, 0, 0, CELL, CELL, CGray)
    Rect(img, 0, 0, CELL, 1, CDark)
    Rect(img, 0, 0, 1, CELL, CDark)
    RETURN img
END FUNCTION


' --- The bits and pieces drawn on top of a square ---

' PaintMine draws the classic spiky sea-mine: a round black body with four
' spikes, and a white glint in the top left so it looks shiny.
SUB PaintMine(img AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image)
    ' The two long spikes, one across and one down.
    Rect(img, 7, 2, 2, 12, CBlack)
    Rect(img, 2, 7, 12, 2, CBlack)
    ' The four short diagonal spikes, drawn one pixel at a time.
    Rect(img, 3, 3, 1, 1, CBlack)
    Rect(img, 4, 4, 1, 1, CBlack)
    Rect(img, 12, 12, 1, 1, CBlack)
    Rect(img, 11, 11, 1, 1, CBlack)
    Rect(img, 12, 3, 1, 1, CBlack)
    Rect(img, 11, 4, 1, 1, CBlack)
    Rect(img, 3, 12, 1, 1, CBlack)
    Rect(img, 4, 11, 1, 1, CBlack)
    ' The round body.
    vector.DrawFilledCircle(img, Sng(8), Sng(8), Sng(4.5), CBlack, FALSE)
    ' The glint, up in the top left where the light would catch it.
    Rect(img, 5, 5, 2, 2, CWhite)
END SUB

' PaintFlag draws the little red pennant on its black pole and stand.
SUB PaintFlag(img AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image, colour AS BOOLEAN)
    DIM fc AS color.RGBA = CRed
    IF NOT colour THEN
        fc = CBlack
    ENDIF
    ' The pennant: a triangle pointing left, widest in the middle.
    Rect(img, 7, 3, 1, 1, fc)
    Rect(img, 6, 4, 2, 1, fc)
    Rect(img, 5, 5, 3, 1, fc)
    Rect(img, 4, 6, 4, 1, fc)
    Rect(img, 5, 7, 3, 1, fc)
    Rect(img, 6, 8, 2, 1, fc)
    Rect(img, 7, 9, 1, 1, fc)
    ' The pole and the two-step stand at the bottom.
    Rect(img, 8, 3, 1, 8, CBlack)
    Rect(img, 6, 11, 5, 1, CBlack)
    Rect(img, 5, 12, 7, 1, CBlack)
END SUB

' PaintCross draws the red X that marks a flag you got wrong.
SUB PaintCross(img AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image)
    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO 9
        Rect(img, 3 + i, 3 + i, 2, 1, CRed)
        Rect(img, 12 - i, 3 + i, 2, 1, CRed)
    NEXT
END SUB


' --- The red LED digits of the counter and the timer ---
'
' A 7-segment display is seven little bars arranged like a figure 8:
'
'        aaaa
'       f    b
'       f    b
'        gggg
'       e    c
'       e    c
'        dddd
'
' To show a digit we simply light up the right combination of bars.

' LedSegs says which bars are lit for one digit. The answer is seven
' characters, one per bar in the order a b c d e f g; "1" means lit.
FUNCTION LedSegs(d AS INTEGER) AS STRING
    SELECT CASE d
        CASE 0
            RETURN "1111110"
        CASE 1
            RETURN "0110000"
        CASE 2
            RETURN "1101101"
        CASE 3
            RETURN "1111001"
        CASE 4
            RETURN "0110011"
        CASE 5
            RETURN "1011011"
        CASE 6
            RETURN "1011111"
        CASE 7
            RETURN "1110000"
        CASE 8
            RETURN "1111111"
        CASE 9
            RETURN "1111011"
    END SELECT
    RETURN "0000001"      ' anything else shows a minus sign (bar g only)
END FUNCTION

' MakeLed builds the picture of one glowing digit on a black background.
FUNCTION MakeLed(d AS INTEGER, colour AS BOOLEAN) AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image
    DIM img AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image = ebiten.NewImage(LEDW, LEDH)
    img.Fill(CBlack)
    DIM on AS color.RGBA = CRed
    IF NOT colour THEN
        on = CWhite
    ENDIF
    DIM s AS STRING = LedSegs(d)
    ' The bars stop one pixel short of each edge so that neighbouring
    ' digits do not run into one another - without that gap, a "1" next
    ' to a "0" looks like one solid blob.
    IF Mid(s, 1, 1) = "1" THEN Rect(img, 3, 0, 7, 3, on)      ' a - top
    IF Mid(s, 2, 1) = "1" THEN Rect(img, 9, 3, 3, 8, on)      ' b - top right
    IF Mid(s, 3, 1) = "1" THEN Rect(img, 9, 13, 3, 7, on)     ' c - bottom right
    IF Mid(s, 4, 1) = "1" THEN Rect(img, 3, 20, 7, 3, on)     ' d - bottom
    IF Mid(s, 5, 1) = "1" THEN Rect(img, 1, 13, 3, 7, on)     ' e - bottom left
    IF Mid(s, 6, 1) = "1" THEN Rect(img, 1, 3, 3, 8, on)      ' f - top left
    IF Mid(s, 7, 1) = "1" THEN Rect(img, 3, 10, 7, 3, on)     ' g - middle
    RETURN img
END FUNCTION


' --- The smiley face button ---

' MakeFace builds one of the four faces on its raised grey button.
'   which = FACE_SMILE / FACE_OOH / FACE_WIN / FACE_DEAD
FUNCTION MakeFace(which AS INTEGER, pressed AS BOOLEAN) AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image
    DIM img AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image = ebiten.NewImage(FACEW, FACEW)
    Rect(img, 0, 0, FACEW, FACEW, CGray)
    IF pressed THEN
        ' A pushed-in button: sunken edge and the face nudged down-right.
        Bevel(img, 0, 0, FACEW, FACEW, 2, FALSE)
    ELSE
        Bevel(img, 0, 0, FACEW, FACEW, 2, TRUE)
    ENDIF

    ' The yellow head: a circle with a black outline.
    DIM cx AS INTEGER = 12
    DIM cy AS INTEGER = 12
    IF pressed THEN
        cx = 13
        cy = 13
    ENDIF
    vector.DrawFilledCircle(img, Sng(cx), Sng(cy), Sng(8), CBlack, FALSE)
    vector.DrawFilledCircle(img, Sng(cx), Sng(cy), Sng(6.8), CYellow, FALSE)

    DIM lx AS INTEGER = cx - 4      ' left eye
    DIM rx AS INTEGER = cx + 2      ' right eye
    DIM ey AS INTEGER = cy - 4      ' eye height

    SELECT CASE which
        CASE FACE_WIN
            ' Sunglasses: two dark lenses joined by a bridge.
            Rect(img, cx - 6, ey, 5, 3, CBlack)
            Rect(img, cx + 1, ey, 5, 3, CBlack)
            Rect(img, cx - 1, ey + 1, 2, 1, CBlack)
            ' A wide grin.
            Rect(img, cx - 2, cy + 4, 4, 1, CBlack)
            Rect(img, cx - 3, cy + 3, 1, 1, CBlack)
            Rect(img, cx + 2, cy + 3, 1, 1, CBlack)
        CASE FACE_DEAD
            ' Two X-shaped eyes.
            DIM i AS INTEGER
            FOR i = 0 TO 3
                Rect(img, lx + i, ey + i, 1, 1, CBlack)
                Rect(img, lx + 3 - i, ey + i, 1, 1, CBlack)
                Rect(img, rx + i, ey + i, 1, 1, CBlack)
                Rect(img, rx + 3 - i, ey + i, 1, 1, CBlack)
            NEXT
            ' An upside-down mouth.
            Rect(img, cx - 2, cy + 3, 4, 1, CBlack)
            Rect(img, cx - 3, cy + 4, 1, 1, CBlack)
            Rect(img, cx + 2, cy + 4, 1, 1, CBlack)
        CASE FACE_OOH
            ' Round staring eyes and a small "o" of a mouth.
            Rect(img, lx + 1, ey, 2, 3, CBlack)
            Rect(img, rx + 1, ey, 2, 3, CBlack)
            Rect(img, cx - 2, cy + 2, 4, 4, CBlack)
            Rect(img, cx - 1, cy + 3, 2, 2, CYellow)
        CASE ELSE
            ' The plain happy face.
            Rect(img, lx + 1, ey, 2, 3, CBlack)
            Rect(img, rx + 1, ey, 2, 3, CBlack)
            Rect(img, cx - 2, cy + 4, 4, 1, CBlack)
            Rect(img, cx - 3, cy + 3, 1, 1, CBlack)
            Rect(img, cx + 2, cy + 3, 1, 1, CBlack)
    END SELECT
    RETURN img
END FUNCTION


' MakeSprites draws every picture the game will ever need. It is called
' once at start-up, and again if the player switches the Color option,
' because that changes what some of the pictures look like.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) MakeSprites()
    g.ImgCovered = MakeCovered()
    g.ImgBlank = MakeBlank()

    ' The numbers 1 to 8, each on a blank square in its own colour.
    DIM nums AS []POINTER TO ebiten.Image
    DIM n AS INTEGER
    FOR n = 1 TO 8
        DIM img AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image = MakeBlank()
        DIM clr AS color.RGBA = GNumClr[n - 1]
        IF NOT g.UseColor THEN
            clr = CBlack
        ENDIF
        DrawGlyph(img, n, 5, 4, clr)
        APPEND(nums, img)
    NEXT
    g.ImgNum = nums

    ' A flag on a covered square.
    g.ImgFlag = MakeCovered()
    PaintFlag(g.ImgFlag, g.UseColor)

    ' A question mark, both on a covered square and on an uncovered one
    ' (the second is what you see while you hold the mouse down on it).
    g.ImgQuery = MakeCovered()
    DrawGlyph(g.ImgQuery, 9, 5, 4, CBlack)
    g.ImgQueryDn = MakeBlank()
    DrawGlyph(g.ImgQueryDn, 9, 5, 4, CBlack)

    ' A mine sitting quietly on an uncovered square.
    g.ImgMine = MakeBlank()
    PaintMine(g.ImgMine)

    ' The mine you actually stepped on: same thing on a red square.
    g.ImgBoom = MakeBlank()
    DIM boomBg AS color.RGBA = CRed
    IF NOT g.UseColor THEN
        boomBg = CDark
    ENDIF
    Rect(g.ImgBoom, 1, 1, CELL - 1, CELL - 1, boomBg)
    PaintMine(g.ImgBoom)

    ' A flag that turned out to be wrong: the mine with a red X over it.
    g.ImgWrong = MakeBlank()
    PaintMine(g.ImgWrong)
    PaintCross(g.ImgWrong)

    ' The eleven LED digits: 0 to 9, plus a minus sign at position 10.
    DIM leds AS []POINTER TO ebiten.Image
    DIM d AS INTEGER
    FOR d = 0 TO 10
        APPEND(leds, MakeLed(d, g.UseColor))
    NEXT
    g.ImgLed = leds

    ' The four faces, plus the "button pushed in" version.
    DIM faces AS []POINTER TO ebiten.Image
    DIM f AS INTEGER
    FOR f = 0 TO 3
        APPEND(faces, MakeFace(f, FALSE))
    NEXT
    g.ImgFace = faces
    g.ImgFaceDn = MakeFace(FACE_SMILE, TRUE)
END SUB


' ======================== 4. SOUND ========================
'
' The game ships with no sound files. Instead we MAKE the sounds, sample by
' sample, when the program starts.
'
' HOW DIGITAL SOUND WORKS
'   Sound is air wobbling. A loudspeaker wobbles the air by pushing its cone
'   out and pulling it back in, and it does whatever a list of numbers tells
'   it to: a big number means "push right out", a big negative number means
'   "pull right in", nought means "stay put".
'
'   The list is read at a fixed speed - 44100 numbers a second, which is
'   quick enough that your ear hears a smooth sound rather than 44100 tiny
'   nudges. Write out "out, out, out... in, in, in..." so that the cone
'   changes direction 440 times a second, and you hear the note A.
'
'   Flipping straight from full out to full in, with nothing in between, is
'   called a SQUARE WAVE. It is the harshest, buzziest sound there is, and
'   it is exactly what games sounded like before sound cards - which makes
'   it perfect here.
'
'   Each number takes two bytes, and we need two of them (left and right ear),
'   so every sample becomes four characters in the string we build.

CONST RATE AS INTEGER = 44100     ' samples per second

' PcmSample turns one loudness number into the four characters that the
' audio system expects: low byte, high byte, twice (once per ear).
FUNCTION PcmSample(v AS INTEGER) AS STRING
    DIM uv AS INTEGER = v
    IF uv < 0 THEN
        uv = uv + 65536           ' negative numbers wrap round, as bytes do
    ENDIF
    ' MOD is the remainder after dividing. 700 MOD 256 is 188, because 700
    ' is two lots of 256 with 188 left over. Splitting a number into "how
    ' many 256s" and "what is left over" is how you chop it into two bytes.
    DIM lo AS INTEGER = uv MOD 256
    DIM hi AS INTEGER = uv / 256
    RETURN Chr(lo) & Chr(hi) & Chr(lo) & Chr(hi)
END FUNCTION

' MakeTone builds a plain beep that fades out towards the end.
'   freq  = pitch in cycles per second (440 is the note A)
'   durMs = how long it lasts, in thousandths of a second
'   vol   = how loud, up to about 20000
FUNCTION MakeTone(freq AS INTEGER, durMs AS INTEGER, vol AS INTEGER) AS STRING
    DIM samples AS INTEGER = RATE * durMs / 1000
    DIM period AS INTEGER = RATE / freq
    IF period < 2 THEN
        period = 2
    ENDIF
    DIM half AS INTEGER = period / 2
    DIM sb AS []STRING
    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO samples - 1
        DIM v AS INTEGER = vol
        IF (i MOD period) >= half THEN
            v = -vol
        ENDIF
        v = v * (samples - i) / samples        ' fade out so it does not click
        APPEND(sb, PcmSample(v))
    NEXT
    RETURN strings.Join(sb, "")
END FUNCTION

' MakeChirp is the same idea, but the pitch slides from one note to another.
FUNCTION MakeChirp(f0 AS INTEGER, f1 AS INTEGER, durMs AS INTEGER, vol AS INTEGER) AS STRING
    DIM samples AS INTEGER = RATE * durMs / 1000
    DIM sb AS []STRING
    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO samples - 1
        DIM freq AS INTEGER = f0 + (f1 - f0) * i / samples
        IF freq < 20 THEN
            freq = 20
        ENDIF
        DIM period AS INTEGER = RATE / freq
        IF period < 2 THEN
            period = 2
        ENDIF
        DIM v AS INTEGER = vol
        IF (i MOD period) >= (period / 2) THEN
            v = -vol
        ENDIF
        v = v * (samples - i) / samples
        APPEND(sb, PcmSample(v))
    NEXT
    RETURN strings.Join(sb, "")
END FUNCTION

' MakeBang builds the explosion: random noise (which is what a bang is)
' that dies away quickly, with a low rumble mixed underneath it.
FUNCTION MakeBang(durMs AS INTEGER, vol AS INTEGER) AS STRING
    DIM samples AS INTEGER = RATE * durMs / 1000
    DIM sb AS []STRING
    DIM hold AS INTEGER = 0
    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO samples - 1
        ' Pick a new random value every few samples. Changing it slowly
        ' makes a deep roar; changing it fast makes a thin hiss.
        IF (i MOD 6) = 0 THEN
            hold = RndInt(2 * vol) - vol
        ENDIF
        DIM rumble AS INTEGER = vol / 3
        IF (i MOD 800) >= 400 THEN
            rumble = -rumble
        ENDIF
        DIM v AS INTEGER = (hold + rumble) * (samples - i) / samples
        APPEND(sb, PcmSample(v))
    NEXT
    RETURN strings.Join(sb, "")
END FUNCTION

' MakePlayer hands a lump of sound data to Ebitengine and gets back
' something we can call .Play() on.
FUNCTION MakePlayer(data AS STRING) AS POINTER TO audio.Player
    IF GAudio = NIL THEN
        RETURN NIL
    ENDIF
    DIM reader AS POINTER TO strings.Reader = strings.NewReader(data)
    DIM player AS POINTER TO audio.Player
    DIM err AS ERROR
    player, err = GAudio.NewPlayer(reader)
    IF err <> NIL THEN
        fmt.Println("Sound unavailable:", err)
        RETURN NIL
    ENDIF
    RETURN player
END FUNCTION

' InitSounds makes all five of the game's sound effects.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) InitSounds()
    ' A soft low click every time squares are uncovered.
    g.SndOpen = MakePlayer(MakeTone(180, 25, 2200))
    ' A crisper, higher click for planting or removing a flag.
    g.SndFlag = MakePlayer(MakeTone(900, 20, 2000))
    ' A quiet tick, once a second, while the clock runs.
    g.SndTick = MakePlayer(MakeTone(1400, 12, 1200))
    ' The explosion.
    g.SndBoom = MakePlayer(MakeBang(600, 9000))
    ' A cheerful rising fanfare for winning.
    g.SndWin = MakePlayer(MakeChirp(392, 784, 140, 4000) & MakeChirp(523, 1046, 140, 4000) & MakeChirp(659, 1318, 320, 4000))
END SUB

' Play starts a sound from the beginning - but only if the player has left
' the Sound option switched on in the Game menu.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) Play(snd AS POINTER TO audio.Player)
    IF NOT g.UseSound THEN
        RETURN
    ENDIF
    IF snd = NIL THEN
        RETURN
    ENDIF
    snd.Rewind()
    snd.Play()
END SUB


' ======================== 5. HIGH SCORES ========================
'
' The original game remembered one best time per difficulty. We keep a full
' table of the best five, with the player's name and the date, and we store
' it in the player's home folder so it survives for good - it does not matter
' which folder the game is started from, and deleting the game does not lose
' the scores.
'
' The file is ordinary text, so you can open it in any editor:
'
'     # DBasic BombSquad high scores
'     beginner=12|Rick|2026-08-22
'     expert=214|Rick|2026-08-19
'
' Custom boards are deliberately NOT recorded: you could make a 9x9 board
' with one mine and "win" in a second, which would not be much of a record.

' ScoreFile works out where to keep the score file.
FUNCTION ScoreFile() AS STRING
    DIM home AS STRING
    DIM err AS ERROR
    home, err = os.UserHomeDir()
    IF err <> NIL OR home = "" THEN
        RETURN "bombsquad.ini"          ' fall back to the current folder
    ENDIF
    RETURN filepath.Join(home, ".dbasic-bombsquad.ini")
END FUNCTION

' LevelKey is the short word used for a difficulty inside the score file.
FUNCTION LevelKey(lvl AS INTEGER) AS STRING
    SELECT CASE lvl
        CASE LV_BEGINNER
            RETURN "beginner"
        CASE LV_INTER
            RETURN "intermediate"
        CASE LV_EXPERT
            RETURN "expert"
    END SELECT
    RETURN "custom"
END FUNCTION

' LevelName is the same thing spelled out for the player to read.
FUNCTION LevelName(lvl AS INTEGER) AS STRING
    SELECT CASE lvl
        CASE LV_BEGINNER
            RETURN "Beginner"
        CASE LV_INTER
            RETURN "Intermediate"
        CASE LV_EXPERT
            RETURN "Expert"
    END SELECT
    RETURN "Custom"
END FUNCTION

' ScoresFor hands back the table belonging to one difficulty.
FUNCTION (g AS POINTER TO Game) ScoresFor(lvl AS INTEGER) AS []ScoreEntry
    SELECT CASE lvl
        CASE LV_BEGINNER
            RETURN g.ScBeg
        CASE LV_INTER
            RETURN g.ScInt
        CASE LV_EXPERT
            RETURN g.ScExp
    END SELECT
    DIM empty AS []ScoreEntry
    RETURN empty
END FUNCTION

' SetScoresFor replaces the table belonging to one difficulty.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) SetScoresFor(lvl AS INTEGER, list AS []ScoreEntry)
    SELECT CASE lvl
        CASE LV_BEGINNER
            g.ScBeg = list
        CASE LV_INTER
            g.ScInt = list
        CASE LV_EXPERT
            g.ScExp = list
    END SELECT
END SUB

' LoadScores reads the score file back in. A missing or damaged file simply
' means "no scores yet" - the game never refuses to start over a bad file.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) LoadScores()
    ' Work out the file name once. Leaving it alone if it is already set
    ' means a test can point the game at a scratch file instead.
    IF g.ScFile = "" THEN
        g.ScFile = ScoreFile()
    ENDIF
    DIM beg AS []ScoreEntry
    DIM med AS []ScoreEntry
    DIM exp AS []ScoreEntry
    g.ScBeg = beg
    g.ScInt = med
    g.ScExp = exp

    IF NOT FileExists(g.ScFile) THEN
        RETURN
    ENDIF

    DIM text AS STRING = ReadFile(g.ScFile)
    DIM lines AS []STRING = strings.Split(text, Chr(10))
    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO Len(lines) - 1
        DIM line AS STRING = Trim(lines[i])
        DIM eq AS INTEGER = Instr(line, "=")
        ' Skip blank lines, comments, and anything without a "=" in it.
        IF line <> "" AND Left(line, 1) <> "#" AND eq >= 2 THEN
            ' Split "beginner=12|Rick|2026-08-22" into a key and a value.
            DIM key AS STRING = LCase(Trim(Left(line, eq - 1)))
            DIM rest AS STRING = Trim(Mid(line, eq + 1, Len(line) - eq))
            DIM parts AS []STRING = strings.Split(rest, "|")
            IF key = "marks" OR key = "color" OR key = "sound" THEN
                ' The three Game-menu options live in the same file.
                DIM on AS BOOLEAN = (Trim(rest) = "1")
                IF key = "marks" THEN
                    g.UseMarks = on
                ELSEIF key = "color" THEN
                    g.UseColor = on
                ELSE
                    g.UseSound = on
                ENDIF
            ELSEIF key = "level" OR key = "rows" OR key = "cols" OR key = "mines" THEN
                ' ...and so does the board you were playing last time.
                DIM nv AS INTEGER = Int(Val(Trim(rest)))
                IF key = "level" THEN
                    g.Level = nv
                ELSEIF key = "rows" THEN
                    g.Rows = nv
                ELSEIF key = "cols" THEN
                    g.Cols = nv
                ELSE
                    g.NMines = nv
                ENDIF
            ELSEIF Len(parts) >= 2 THEN
                DIM secs AS INTEGER
                DIM cerr AS ERROR
                secs, cerr = strconv.Atoi(Trim(parts[0]))
                IF cerr = NIL THEN
                    DIM e AS ScoreEntry
                    e.Secs = secs
                    e.Who = Trim(parts[1])
                    e.DateStr = ""
                    IF Len(parts) >= 3 THEN
                        e.DateStr = Trim(parts[2])
                    ENDIF
                    SELECT CASE key
                        CASE "beginner"
                            g.ScBeg = APPEND(g.ScBeg, e)
                        CASE "intermediate"
                            g.ScInt = APPEND(g.ScInt, e)
                        CASE "expert"
                            g.ScExp = APPEND(g.ScExp, e)
                    END SELECT
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        ENDIF
    NEXT
END SUB

' OnOff turns TRUE and FALSE into the "1" and "0" stored in the file.
FUNCTION OnOff(b AS BOOLEAN) AS STRING
    IF b THEN
        RETURN "1"
    ENDIF
    RETURN "0"
END FUNCTION

' SaveScores writes the three tables (and the menu options) back out to disk.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) SaveScores()
    DIM sb AS []STRING
    APPEND(sb, "# DBasic BombSquad high scores")
    APPEND(sb, "# level=seconds|name|date")
    APPEND(sb, "marks=" & OnOff(g.UseMarks))
    APPEND(sb, "color=" & OnOff(g.UseColor))
    APPEND(sb, "sound=" & OnOff(g.UseSound))
    APPEND(sb, "level=" & Str(g.Level))
    APPEND(sb, "rows=" & Str(g.Rows))
    APPEND(sb, "cols=" & Str(g.Cols))
    APPEND(sb, "mines=" & Str(g.NMines))
    DIM lvl AS INTEGER
    FOR lvl = LV_BEGINNER TO LV_EXPERT
        DIM list AS []ScoreEntry = g.ScoresFor(lvl)
        DIM i AS INTEGER
        FOR i = 0 TO Len(list) - 1
            APPEND(sb, LevelKey(lvl) & "=" & Str(list[i].Secs) & "|" & list[i].Who & "|" & list[i].DateStr)
        NEXT
    NEXT
    WriteFile(g.ScFile, strings.Join(sb, Chr(10)) & Chr(10))
END SUB

' ScoreRank asks "if I finished this level in this many seconds, where would
' I come in the table?"
'
' The table is already sorted with the fastest time at the top, so we walk
' down it from the top and stop at the first time we beat - that is the slot
' we would push into, and everybody below shuffles down one. If we never
' beat anybody but there is still a spare row, we go on the end. If we beat
' nobody and the table is full, we did not make it: the answer is -1.
FUNCTION (g AS POINTER TO Game) ScoreRank(lvl AS INTEGER, secs AS INTEGER) AS INTEGER
    ' Custom boards are not ranked.
    IF lvl = LV_CUSTOM THEN
        RETURN -1
    ENDIF
    DIM list AS []ScoreEntry = g.ScoresFor(lvl)
    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO Len(list) - 1
        IF secs < list[i].Secs THEN
            RETURN i
        ENDIF
    NEXT
    ' Not faster than anyone, but there may still be a free slot.
    IF Len(list) < TOP_N THEN
        RETURN Len(list)
    ENDIF
    RETURN -1
END FUNCTION

' InsertScore slots a new entry into a table at the given position and
' throws away anything that falls off the bottom.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) InsertScore(lvl AS INTEGER, rank AS INTEGER, e AS ScoreEntry)
    DIM old AS []ScoreEntry = g.ScoresFor(lvl)
    DIM fresh AS []ScoreEntry
    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO Len(old) - 1
        IF i = rank THEN
            APPEND(fresh, e)
        ENDIF
        APPEND(fresh, old[i])
    NEXT
    ' If the new time goes on the end, the loop above never added it.
    IF rank >= Len(old) THEN
        APPEND(fresh, e)
    ENDIF
    ' Keep only the best few.
    IF Len(fresh) > TOP_N THEN
        fresh = fresh[0:TOP_N]
    ENDIF
    g.SetScoresFor(lvl, fresh)
    g.SaveScores()
END SUB

' There is deliberately no way to wipe the high score tables from inside the
' game. A record you can clear with one stray click is not much of a record,
' and the whole point of these tables is that they last. If you really do
' want to start again, delete the file named in Help > About.


' ======================== 6. THE MINEFIELD ========================
'
' The board is stored as three plain lists, one entry per square, running
' left to right and then top to bottom. The square in row r, column c is at
' position r * Cols + c. Storing a grid as a single flat list like this is
' an old trick and it keeps the code much simpler than a grid of grids.

' WHY r * g.Cols + c?
'
' A list is one long line of boxes. A board is a rectangle. To store the
' rectangle in the line we simply read the board the way you read a page:
' all of row 0, then all of row 1, and so on.
'
' So to find row 3, column 2 on a 9-wide board: skip three whole rows, which
' is 3 x 9 = 27 boxes, then count 2 more along. Box 29. That is all the sum
' r * g.Cols + c is doing - "skip that many whole rows, then count across".
'
' Going the other way, from a box number back to a row and column:
'     row    = box / g.Cols        how many whole rows fit in front of it
'     column = box MOD g.Cols      what is left over after those rows
'
' You will see all three of these sums written out in full below rather than
' tucked away in a function, because once you have read this note the
' arithmetic tells you more than a name would.

' Relayout works out where everything sits on screen for the current board
' size, and resizes the window to match. It is called whenever the player
' picks a different difficulty.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) Relayout()
    DIM innerW AS INTEGER = g.Cols * CELL
    ' How big the board needs the window to be. Working down the picture:
    ' the menu bar, the raised outer border, a grey gap, the sunken frame,
    ' the header, another gap and frame, the squares, and the border again.
    g.ScrW = innerW + 2 * (OUTER + GAP + FRAME)
    g.ScrH = MENUH + OUTER + GAP + FRAME + HDRH + FRAME + GAP + FRAME + g.Rows * CELL + FRAME + GAP + OUTER
    g.UpdateView()
END SUB

' PlaceMines scatters the mines at random over the whole board.
'
' It works like dealing cards blindfolded: point at a square at random, and
' if there is already a mine there, point again. Keep going until you have
' put down as many as you meant to. Because we only ever count a square when
' it was empty, two mines can never end up stacked on top of one another.
'
' (On a board that is nearly all mines this does a lot of pointing at
' squares that are already taken - but "nearly all mines" only happens if
' somebody types a silly number into the Custom box, and even then it
' finishes in the blink of an eye.)
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) PlaceMines()
    DIM total AS INTEGER = g.Rows * g.Cols
    DIM placed AS INTEGER = 0
    WHILE placed < g.NMines
        DIM at AS INTEGER = RndInt(total)
        IF NOT g.Mine[at] THEN
            g.Mine[at] = TRUE
            placed = placed + 1
        ENDIF
    WEND
END SUB

' ComputeAdj fills in, for every square, how many of its eight neighbours
' hold a mine. That number is what gets printed on the square when you
' uncover it.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) ComputeAdj()
    DIM r AS INTEGER
    DIM c AS INTEGER
    FOR r = 0 TO g.Rows - 1
        FOR c = 0 TO g.Cols - 1
            DIM n AS INTEGER = 0
            DIM dr AS INTEGER
            DIM dc AS INTEGER
            FOR dr = -1 TO 1
                FOR dc = -1 TO 1
                    DIM rr AS INTEGER = r + dr
                    DIM cc AS INTEGER = c + dc
                    ' Stay inside the board, and do not count ourselves.
                    IF rr >= 0 AND rr < g.Rows AND cc >= 0 AND cc < g.Cols THEN
                        IF NOT (dr = 0 AND dc = 0) THEN
                            IF g.Mine[rr * g.Cols + cc] THEN
                                n = n + 1
                            ENDIF
                        ENDIF
                    ENDIF
                NEXT
            NEXT
            g.Adj[r * g.Cols + c] = n
        NEXT
    NEXT
END SUB

' NewBoard throws away the old game and deals a brand new one.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) NewBoard(rows AS INTEGER, cols AS INTEGER, mines AS INTEGER, lvl AS INTEGER)
    g.Rows = rows
    g.Cols = cols
    g.NMines = mines
    g.Level = lvl

    ' Fresh, empty lists - one entry per square. REDIM is BASIC for "make
    ' this list exactly that long", and the new entries all start out as
    ' nought, or FALSE, which is precisely what an untouched board is.
    DIM total AS INTEGER = rows * cols
    DIM mineList AS []BOOLEAN
    DIM stList AS []INTEGER
    DIM adjList AS []INTEGER
    REDIM mineList(total) AS BOOLEAN
    REDIM stList(total) AS INTEGER
    REDIM adjList(total) AS INTEGER
    g.Mine = mineList
    g.St = stList
    g.Adj = adjList

    g.Started = FALSE
    g.Over = FALSE
    g.Won = FALSE
    g.BoomIdx = -1
    g.NShown = 0
    g.NFlags = 0
    g.Secs = 0
    g.Ticks = 0
    g.Face = FACE_SMILE
    g.PressIdx = -1
    g.Chording = FALSE
    g.Swallow = FALSE
    g.FaceDown = FALSE

    g.PlaceMines()
    g.ComputeAdj()
    g.Relayout()
END SUB

' StartLevel deals a new board at one of the three standard difficulties.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) StartLevel(lvl AS INTEGER)
    SELECT CASE lvl
        CASE LV_BEGINNER
            g.NewBoard(9, 9, 10, LV_BEGINNER)
        CASE LV_INTER
            g.NewBoard(16, 16, 40, LV_INTER)
        CASE ELSE
            g.NewBoard(16, 30, 99, LV_EXPERT)
    END SELECT
    g.SaveScores()          ' remember this choice for next time
END SUB

' BeginPlay runs on the very first square the player uncovers.
'
' Losing on your very first click would be miserable, so the game cheats -
' and it cheats in a wonderfully lazy way that the original invented. It
' does not think carefully about where to put the mines; it lays them out at
' random like always, and then, if your first click lands on one, it just
' picks that mine up and drops it on the first free square counting from the
' top left corner. Moving a mine changes the neighbour counts, so those all
' have to be worked out again afterwards.
'
' A side effect is that boards laid this way have very slightly more mines
' near the top left than anywhere else. We have kept the quirk.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) BeginPlay(at AS INTEGER)
    g.Started = TRUE
    g.Secs = 1              ' the clock shows 001 the moment you start
    g.Ticks = 0

    IF g.Mine[at] THEN
        DIM i AS INTEGER
        FOR i = 0 TO g.Rows * g.Cols - 1
            IF NOT g.Mine[i] AND i <> at THEN
                g.Mine[i] = TRUE
                g.Mine[at] = FALSE
                EXIT FOR
            ENDIF
        NEXT
        g.ComputeAdj()      ' the counts all change when a mine moves
    ENDIF
END SUB

' Lose ends the game: the mine that was stepped on is drawn on a red
' background and every other mine is uncovered, exactly as the original did.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) Lose(at AS INTEGER)
    g.Over = TRUE
    g.Won = FALSE
    g.BoomIdx = at
    g.Face = FACE_DEAD
    g.Play(g.SndBoom)
END SUB

' Win ends the game the happy way. Any mine you had not got round to
' flagging is flagged for you, so the counter finishes on zero.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) Win()
    g.Over = TRUE
    g.Won = TRUE
    g.Face = FACE_WIN
    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO g.Rows * g.Cols - 1
        IF g.Mine[i] AND g.St[i] <> ST_FLAG THEN
            g.St[i] = ST_FLAG
        ENDIF
    NEXT
    g.NFlags = g.NMines
    g.Play(g.SndWin)

    ' Did that beat one of the recorded times? If so, ask for a name.
    DIM rank AS INTEGER = g.ScoreRank(g.Level, g.Secs)
    IF rank >= 0 THEN
        g.NameLvl = g.Level
        g.NameRank = rank
        g.NameBuf = ""
        g.Dlg = DLG_NAME
        g.Focus = 0
    ENDIF
END SUB

' CheckWin asks "is every square that is not a mine now uncovered?"
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) CheckWin()
    IF g.NShown = g.Rows * g.Cols - g.NMines THEN
        g.Win()
    ENDIF
END SUB

' Reveal uncovers one square - and, if that square turns out to have no
' mines at all around it, its neighbours too, and their neighbours, and so
' on. That is the satisfying cascade that clears half the board in one click.
'
' HOW THE CASCADE WORKS
'   Imagine you are handed a jotter with one square written on it. You rub
'   out the top entry, uncover that square, and if it turns out to be blank
'   you write its eight neighbours onto the jotter. Then you do the top one
'   again. You keep going until the jotter is empty - and by then the whole
'   blank region has opened up, plus a ring of numbers around the edge of it.
'
'   The jotter is the list called "todo" below. This is called a flood fill,
'   and it is exactly what the paint bucket in a drawing program does.
'
'   Why a jotter and not just "uncover my neighbours, and tell them to
'   uncover theirs"? Because on a big empty board that would be thousands of
'   routines all waiting on each other, and the program would fall over. A
'   list uses the same tiny amount of memory however big the region is.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) Reveal(at AS INTEGER)
    IF g.Over THEN
        RETURN
    ENDIF
    ' Flags protect a square from being uncovered by accident.
    IF g.St[at] = ST_REVEALED OR g.St[at] = ST_FLAG THEN
        RETURN
    ENDIF

    IF NOT g.Started THEN
        g.BeginPlay(at)
    ENDIF

    IF g.Mine[at] THEN
        g.St[at] = ST_REVEALED
        g.Lose(at)
        RETURN
    ENDIF

    DIM todo AS []INTEGER
    APPEND(todo, at)
    WHILE Len(todo) > 0
        ' Take the last square off the to-do list.
        DIM cur AS INTEGER = todo[Len(todo) - 1]
        todo = todo[0:Len(todo) - 1]

        ' It may already have been dealt with, or be flagged.
        IF g.St[cur] <> ST_REVEALED AND g.St[cur] <> ST_FLAG THEN
            g.St[cur] = ST_REVEALED
            g.NShown = g.NShown + 1

            ' A blank square (no mines nearby) opens up its neighbours.
            IF g.Adj[cur] = 0 THEN
                DIM r AS INTEGER = cur / g.Cols
                DIM c AS INTEGER = cur MOD g.Cols
                DIM dr AS INTEGER
                DIM dc AS INTEGER
                FOR dr = -1 TO 1
                    FOR dc = -1 TO 1
                        DIM rr AS INTEGER = r + dr
                        DIM cc AS INTEGER = c + dc
                        IF rr >= 0 AND rr < g.Rows AND cc >= 0 AND cc < g.Cols THEN
                            DIM ni AS INTEGER = rr * g.Cols + cc
                            IF g.St[ni] <> ST_REVEALED AND g.St[ni] <> ST_FLAG THEN
                                APPEND(todo, ni)
                            ENDIF
                        ENDIF
                    NEXT
                NEXT
            ENDIF
        ENDIF
    WEND

    g.Play(g.SndOpen)
    g.CheckWin()
END SUB

' Chord is the expert's shortcut. Press both mouse buttons on an uncovered
' number and, if you have already planted exactly that many flags around it,
' every other neighbour is uncovered in one go.
'
' It is a gamble: if one of those flags is in the wrong place you will
' uncover a mine and lose. The original game worked the same way.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) Chord(at AS INTEGER)
    IF g.Over OR NOT g.Started THEN
        RETURN
    ENDIF
    IF g.St[at] <> ST_REVEALED OR g.Adj[at] = 0 THEN
        RETURN
    ENDIF

    DIM r AS INTEGER = at / g.Cols
    DIM c AS INTEGER = at MOD g.Cols
    DIM flags AS INTEGER = 0
    DIM dr AS INTEGER
    DIM dc AS INTEGER

    ' First count the flags around it.
    FOR dr = -1 TO 1
        FOR dc = -1 TO 1
            DIM rr AS INTEGER = r + dr
            DIM cc AS INTEGER = c + dc
            IF rr >= 0 AND rr < g.Rows AND cc >= 0 AND cc < g.Cols THEN
                IF g.St[rr * g.Cols + cc] = ST_FLAG THEN
                    flags = flags + 1
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        NEXT
    NEXT

    ' Only go ahead if the count is exactly right.
    IF flags <> g.Adj[at] THEN
        RETURN
    ENDIF

    FOR dr = -1 TO 1
        FOR dc = -1 TO 1
            DIM rr2 AS INTEGER = r + dr
            DIM cc2 AS INTEGER = c + dc
            IF rr2 >= 0 AND rr2 < g.Rows AND cc2 >= 0 AND cc2 < g.Cols THEN
                DIM ni AS INTEGER = rr2 * g.Cols + cc2
                IF g.St[ni] = ST_COVERED OR g.St[ni] = ST_QUERY THEN
                    g.Reveal(ni)
                ENDIF
            ENDIF
        NEXT
    NEXT
END SUB

' Mark is what the right mouse button does: it cycles a square round a
' little loop of markings, like clicking a pen.
'
'     nothing  ->  flag  ->  question mark  ->  nothing  ->  ...
'
' The flag means "I am sure there is a mine here" and protects the square
' from being uncovered by accident. The question mark means "I think there
' might be" and protects nothing - you can still click it open.
'
' With the Marks option switched off the middle step is skipped, so it is
' just flag, no flag, flag, no flag. That is how most people play.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) Mark(at AS INTEGER)
    IF g.Over THEN
        RETURN
    ENDIF
    SELECT CASE g.St[at]
        CASE ST_COVERED
            g.St[at] = ST_FLAG
            g.NFlags = g.NFlags + 1
            g.Play(g.SndFlag)
        CASE ST_FLAG
            g.NFlags = g.NFlags - 1
            IF g.UseMarks THEN
                g.St[at] = ST_QUERY
            ELSE
                g.St[at] = ST_COVERED
            ENDIF
            g.Play(g.SndFlag)
        CASE ST_QUERY
            g.St[at] = ST_COVERED
            g.Play(g.SndFlag)
    END SELECT
END SUB


' ======================== 7. MENUS AND INPUT ========================

' --- Menu geometry ---
' These say how big the drop-down menus are. Everything is worked out from
' the character size so nothing has to be measured by hand.
CONST ITEMH AS INTEGER = 16      ' height of one menu line
CONST SEPH AS INTEGER = 6        ' height of a dividing line
CONST LABELX AS INTEGER = 22     ' how far in the words start (room for a tick)
CONST PADR AS INTEGER = 16       ' blank space kept on the right

' MkItem builds an ordinary menu line.
FUNCTION MkItem(label AS STRING, accel AS STRING, id AS INTEGER, checked AS BOOLEAN) AS MenuItem
    DIM m AS MenuItem
    m.Label = label
    m.Accel = accel
    m.Id = id
    m.Sep = FALSE
    m.Checked = checked
    RETURN m
END FUNCTION

' MkSep builds a dividing line.
FUNCTION MkSep() AS MenuItem
    DIM m AS MenuItem
    m.Sep = TRUE
    m.Id = MI_NONE
    RETURN m
END FUNCTION

' TitleW is how wide a menu bar heading is.
FUNCTION TitleW(label AS STRING) AS INTEGER
    RETURN Len(label) * CHW + 12
END FUNCTION

' TitleX is where a menu bar heading starts. There are only two of them.
FUNCTION TitleX(i AS INTEGER) AS INTEGER
    IF i = 0 THEN
        RETURN 2
    ENDIF
    RETURN 2 + TitleW("Game")
END FUNCTION

' DropH is the total height of the drop-down that is open.
FUNCTION DropH(g AS POINTER TO Game) AS INTEGER
    DIM h AS INTEGER = 2 * FRAME
    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO Len(g.MenuItems) - 1
        IF g.MenuItems[i].Sep THEN
            h = h + SEPH
        ELSE
            h = h + ITEMH
        ENDIF
    NEXT
    RETURN h
END FUNCTION

' ItemY is how far down the drop-down the given line starts.
FUNCTION ItemY(g AS POINTER TO Game, n AS INTEGER) AS INTEGER
    DIM y AS INTEGER = MENUH + FRAME
    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO n - 1
        IF g.MenuItems[i].Sep THEN
            y = y + SEPH
        ELSE
            y = y + ITEMH
        ENDIF
    NEXT
    RETURN y
END FUNCTION

' MenuHit says which menu line the mouse is over, or -1 for none.
FUNCTION MenuHit(g AS POINTER TO Game, mx AS INTEGER, my AS INTEGER) AS INTEGER
    IF mx < g.MenuX OR mx >= g.MenuX + g.MenuW THEN
        RETURN -1
    ENDIF
    DIM y AS INTEGER = MENUH + FRAME
    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO Len(g.MenuItems) - 1
        DIM h AS INTEGER = ITEMH
        IF g.MenuItems[i].Sep THEN
            h = SEPH
        ENDIF
        IF my >= y AND my < y + h AND NOT g.MenuItems[i].Sep THEN
            RETURN i
        ENDIF
        y = y + h
    NEXT
    RETURN -1
END FUNCTION

' OpenMenu drops down one of the two menus. The lines are built fresh every
' time so that the ticks next to Beginner, Marks, Sound and so on always
' show what is actually switched on right now.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) OpenMenu(which AS INTEGER)
    DIM items AS []MenuItem
    IF which = 0 THEN
        APPEND(items, MkItem("New", "F2", MI_NEW, FALSE))
        APPEND(items, MkSep())
        APPEND(items, MkItem("Beginner", "", MI_BEG, g.Level = LV_BEGINNER))
        APPEND(items, MkItem("Intermediate", "", MI_INT, g.Level = LV_INTER))
        APPEND(items, MkItem("Expert", "", MI_EXP, g.Level = LV_EXPERT))
        APPEND(items, MkItem("Custom...", "", MI_CUSTOM, g.Level = LV_CUSTOM))
        APPEND(items, MkSep())
        APPEND(items, MkItem("Marks (?)", "", MI_MARKS, g.UseMarks))
        APPEND(items, MkItem("Color", "", MI_COLOR, g.UseColor))
        APPEND(items, MkItem("Sound", "", MI_SOUND, g.UseSound))
        APPEND(items, MkSep())
        APPEND(items, MkItem("Best Times...", "", MI_TIMES, FALSE))
        APPEND(items, MkSep())
        APPEND(items, MkItem("Exit", "", MI_EXIT, FALSE))
    ELSE
        APPEND(items, MkItem("Contents", "F1", MI_HELP, FALSE))
        APPEND(items, MkItem("How to play", "", MI_HELP, FALSE))
        APPEND(items, MkSep())
        APPEND(items, MkItem("About BombSquad...", "", MI_ABOUT, FALSE))
    ENDIF
    g.MenuItems = items
    g.MenuOpen = which
    g.MenuHot = -1
    g.MenuX = TitleX(which)

    ' Make the drop-down wide enough for its longest line.
    DIM w AS INTEGER = 100
    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO Len(items) - 1
        DIM need AS INTEGER = LABELX + Len(items[i].Label) * CHW + PADR
        IF items[i].Accel <> "" THEN
            need = need + Len(items[i].Accel) * CHW + 12
        ENDIF
        IF need > w THEN
            w = need
        ENDIF
    NEXT
    g.MenuW = w
END SUB

' CloseMenu puts the drop-down away.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) CloseMenu()
    g.MenuOpen = -1
    g.MenuHot = -1
END SUB

' Restart deals a new board of the same size as the one being played.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) Restart()
    g.NewBoard(g.Rows, g.Cols, g.NMines, g.Level)
END SUB

' DoCommand carries out whatever the player picked from a menu.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) DoCommand(id AS INTEGER)
    SELECT CASE id
        CASE MI_NEW
            g.Restart()
        CASE MI_BEG
            g.StartLevel(LV_BEGINNER)
        CASE MI_INT
            g.StartLevel(LV_INTER)
        CASE MI_EXP
            g.StartLevel(LV_EXPERT)
        CASE MI_CUSTOM
            ' Fill the boxes with the board we are playing now.
            g.FldRows = Str(g.Rows)
            g.FldCols = Str(g.Cols)
            g.FldMines = Str(g.NMines)
            g.Dlg = DLG_CUSTOM
            g.Focus = 0
        CASE MI_MARKS
            g.UseMarks = NOT g.UseMarks
            ' Switching marks off clears any question marks already placed.
            IF NOT g.UseMarks THEN
                DIM i AS INTEGER
                FOR i = 0 TO g.Rows * g.Cols - 1
                    IF g.St[i] = ST_QUERY THEN
                        g.St[i] = ST_COVERED
                    ENDIF
                NEXT
            ENDIF
            g.SaveScores()
        CASE MI_COLOR
            g.UseColor = NOT g.UseColor
            g.MakeSprites()          ' the pictures change colour with it
            g.SaveScores()
        CASE MI_SOUND
            g.UseSound = NOT g.UseSound
            g.SaveScores()
        CASE MI_TIMES
            g.Dlg = DLG_TIMES
        CASE MI_HELP
            g.Dlg = DLG_HELP
        CASE MI_ABOUT
            g.Dlg = DLG_ABOUT
        CASE MI_EXIT
            g.Quit = TRUE
    END SELECT
END SUB

' --- Typing ---
'
' A keyboard does not hand us letters, it hands us KEYS. Key number 12 is
' not the letter "L", it is "the twelfth key", and it is up to us to decide
' what that means. So we keep two lists side by side: the keys we care
' about, and the character each one stands for. Looking up key number 12 in
' the first list tells us where to look in the second.

DIM GKeyList AS []ebiten.Key = []ebiten.Key{ _
    ebiten.Key0, ebiten.Key1, ebiten.Key2, ebiten.Key3, ebiten.Key4, _
    ebiten.Key5, ebiten.Key6, ebiten.Key7, ebiten.Key8, ebiten.Key9, _
    ebiten.KeyA, ebiten.KeyB, ebiten.KeyC, ebiten.KeyD, ebiten.KeyE, _
    ebiten.KeyF, ebiten.KeyG, ebiten.KeyH, ebiten.KeyI, ebiten.KeyJ, _
    ebiten.KeyK, ebiten.KeyL, ebiten.KeyM, ebiten.KeyN, ebiten.KeyO, _
    ebiten.KeyP, ebiten.KeyQ, ebiten.KeyR, ebiten.KeyS, ebiten.KeyT, _
    ebiten.KeyU, ebiten.KeyV, ebiten.KeyW, ebiten.KeyX, ebiten.KeyY, _
    ebiten.KeyZ, ebiten.KeySpace, ebiten.KeyMinus, ebiten.KeyPeriod}

' The character each key in the list above stands for.
CONST KEYCHARS AS STRING = "0123456789ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ -."

' KeyChar turns a key into the character it types, or "" if it is not one
' of the keys we accept.
FUNCTION KeyChar(k AS ebiten.Key) AS STRING
    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO Len(GKeyList) - 1
        IF GKeyList[i] = k THEN
            RETURN Mid(KEYCHARS, i + 1, 1)
        ENDIF
    NEXT
    RETURN ""
END FUNCTION

' ShiftHeld says whether either Shift key is down, so we know whether the
' player wants a capital letter.
FUNCTION ShiftHeld() AS BOOLEAN
    IF ebiten.IsKeyPressed(ebiten.KeyShiftLeft) THEN
        RETURN TRUE
    ENDIF
    RETURN ebiten.IsKeyPressed(ebiten.KeyShiftRight)
END FUNCTION

' TypedInto adds whatever was just typed onto the end of a piece of text.
'   digitsOnly = TRUE for the Custom Field boxes, which only take numbers
'   maxLen     = how many characters will fit in the box
FUNCTION TypedInto(s AS STRING, digitsOnly AS BOOLEAN, maxLen AS INTEGER) AS STRING
    DIM out AS STRING = s

    ' Backspace rubs out the last character.
    IF inpututil.IsKeyJustPressed(ebiten.KeyBackspace) THEN
        IF Len(out) > 0 THEN
            out = Left(out, Len(out) - 1)
        ENDIF
    ENDIF

    DIM keys AS []ebiten.Key
    keys = inpututil.AppendJustPressedKeys(keys)
    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO Len(keys) - 1
        DIM ch AS STRING = KeyChar(keys[i])
        IF ch <> "" AND Len(out) < maxLen THEN
            IF digitsOnly THEN
                ' Only 0 to 9 get through.
                IF ch >= "0" AND ch <= "9" THEN
                    out = out & ch
                ENDIF
            ELSE
                ' Letters come out lower case unless Shift is held.
                IF NOT ShiftHeld() AND ch >= "A" AND ch <= "Z" THEN
                    ch = LCase(ch)
                ENDIF
                out = out & ch
            ENDIF
        ENDIF
    NEXT
    RETURN out
END FUNCTION


' --- Dialog box geometry ---
'
' Every dialog is a grey panel floating in the middle of the window with a
' blue title bar and one or two buttons along the bottom. These little
' functions say exactly where everything goes, and both the drawing code
' and the clicking code use them, so the two can never disagree.

CONST TITLEH AS INTEGER = 18     ' height of a dialog's blue title bar
CONST BTNW AS INTEGER = 80       ' a push button
CONST BTNH AS INTEGER = 20
CONST FLDW AS INTEGER = 52       ' a box you type numbers into
CONST FLDH AS INTEGER = 18

FUNCTION DlgW(g AS POINTER TO Game) AS INTEGER
    SELECT CASE g.Dlg
        CASE DLG_CUSTOM
            RETURN 262
        CASE DLG_TIMES
            ' 12 pixels of margin, three columns, and 12 again on the right.
            RETURN 12 + 2 * SCORE_COLGAP + SCORE_COLW + 12
        CASE DLG_NAME
            RETURN 330
        CASE DLG_ABOUT
            RETURN 288
        CASE DLG_HELP
            RETURN 392
    END SELECT
    RETURN 200
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION DlgHt(g AS POINTER TO Game) AS INTEGER
    SELECT CASE g.Dlg
        CASE DLG_CUSTOM
            RETURN 148
        CASE DLG_TIMES
            RETURN 208
        CASE DLG_NAME
            RETURN 166
        CASE DLG_ABOUT
            RETURN 184
        CASE DLG_HELP
            RETURN 322
    END SELECT
    RETURN 120
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION DlgX(g AS POINTER TO Game) AS INTEGER
    RETURN (g.ViewW - DlgW(g)) / 2
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION DlgY(g AS POINTER TO Game) AS INTEGER
    RETURN (g.ViewH - DlgHt(g)) / 2
END FUNCTION

' BtnX places the buttons along the bottom, counting from the right:
' button 0 is the rightmost, button 1 sits to its left, and so on.
FUNCTION BtnX(g AS POINTER TO Game, n AS INTEGER) AS INTEGER
    RETURN DlgX(g) + DlgW(g) - 10 - (n + 1) * BTNW - n * 8
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION BtnY(g AS POINTER TO Game) AS INTEGER
    RETURN DlgY(g) + DlgHt(g) - 10 - BTNH
END FUNCTION

' FldX and FldY place the three number boxes in the Custom Field dialog.
FUNCTION FldX(g AS POINTER TO Game) AS INTEGER
    RETURN DlgX(g) + 86
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION FldY(g AS POINTER TO Game, n AS INTEGER) AS INTEGER
    RETURN DlgY(g) + TITLEH + 12 + n * 26
END FUNCTION

' NameTooLong says whether a typed high score name will not fit in the Best
' Times table. Leading and trailing spaces do not count, because they are
' stripped before the name is filed.
FUNCTION NameTooLong(nm AS STRING) AS BOOLEAN
    RETURN Len(Trim(nm)) > NAME_CHARS
END FUNCTION

' NameWarning is the wording shown under the box when that happens. It also
' shows exactly what WILL appear, so there is no guessing.
FUNCTION NameWarning(nm AS STRING) AS STRING
    RETURN "Too long: only " & Chr(34) & Left(Trim(nm), NAME_CHARS) & Chr(34) & " will be shown"
END FUNCTION

' HitRect answers the question every mouse click asks: is this point inside
' that rectangle?
FUNCTION HitRect(mx AS INTEGER, my AS INTEGER, x AS INTEGER, y AS INTEGER, w AS INTEGER, h AS INTEGER) AS BOOLEAN
    IF mx < x OR mx >= x + w THEN
        RETURN FALSE
    ENDIF
    IF my < y OR my >= y + h THEN
        RETURN FALSE
    ENDIF
    RETURN TRUE
END FUNCTION

' --- Where the header bits are ---

FUNCTION FaceX(g AS POINTER TO Game) AS INTEGER
    RETURN g.HdrX + (g.Cols * CELL) / 2 - FACEW / 2
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION FaceY(g AS POINTER TO Game) AS INTEGER
    RETURN g.HdrY + (HDRH - FACEW) / 2
END FUNCTION

' CellAt says which square the mouse is over, or -1 if it is not over the
' minefield at all.
FUNCTION CellAt(g AS POINTER TO Game, x AS INTEGER, y AS INTEGER) AS INTEGER
    IF x < g.GX OR x >= g.GX + g.Cols * CELL THEN
        RETURN -1
    ENDIF
    IF y < g.GY OR y >= g.GY + g.Rows * CELL THEN
        RETURN -1
    ENDIF
    DIM c AS INTEGER = (x - g.GX) / CELL
    DIM r AS INTEGER = (y - g.GY) / CELL
    RETURN r * g.Cols + c
END FUNCTION

' TitleHit says which menu bar heading the mouse is over, or -1.
FUNCTION TitleHit(mx AS INTEGER, my AS INTEGER) AS INTEGER
    IF my < 0 OR my >= MENUH THEN
        RETURN -1
    ENDIF
    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO 1
        DIM label AS STRING = "Game"
        IF i = 1 THEN
            label = "Help"
        ENDIF
        IF mx >= TitleX(i) AND mx < TitleX(i) + TitleW(label) THEN
            RETURN i
        ENDIF
    NEXT
    RETURN -1
END FUNCTION

' UpdateView keeps the window the right size.
'
' Normally the window is exactly as big as the board. But a Beginner board
' is tiny - 168 pixels across - and something like the Best Times table
' simply will not fit inside it. Real Windows got away with this because a
' dialog is its own separate window that can hang over the edge of the one
' underneath. We only have the one window, so instead it grows for as long
' as the dialog is up and shrinks back the moment it closes.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) UpdateView()
    DIM w AS INTEGER = g.ScrW
    DIM h AS INTEGER = g.ScrH
    IF g.Dlg <> DLG_NONE THEN
        DIM needW AS INTEGER = DlgW(g) + 16
        DIM needH AS INTEGER = DlgHt(g) + 16 + MENUH
        IF needW > w THEN
            w = needW
        ENDIF
        IF needH > h THEN
            h = needH
        ENDIF
    ENDIF

    ' If the window ended up bigger than the board, put the board in the
    ' middle of it rather than leaving it stranded in the top left corner.
    g.OX = (w - g.ScrW) / 2
    g.OY = (h - g.ScrH) / 2

    ' Everything on the board is positioned relative to those offsets, so
    ' this is the one place that needs to know about them.
    g.HdrX = g.OX + OUTER + GAP + FRAME
    g.HdrY = g.OY + MENUH + OUTER + GAP + FRAME
    g.GX = g.OX + OUTER + GAP + FRAME
    g.GY = g.HdrY + HDRH + FRAME + GAP + FRAME

    IF w <> g.ViewW OR h <> g.ViewH THEN
        g.ViewW = w
        g.ViewH = h
        ebiten.SetWindowSize(w * g.Zoom, h * g.Zoom)
    ENDIF
END SUB

' ApplyCustom reads the three boxes in the Custom Field dialog and starts a
' board that size. Silly numbers are quietly pulled back into range, which
' is what the original did too.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) ApplyCustom()
    DIM rows AS INTEGER = Int(Val(g.FldRows))
    DIM cols AS INTEGER = Int(Val(g.FldCols))
    DIM mines AS INTEGER = Int(Val(g.FldMines))

    IF rows < MIN_ROWS THEN
        rows = MIN_ROWS
    ENDIF
    IF rows > MAX_ROWS THEN
        rows = MAX_ROWS
    ENDIF
    IF cols < MIN_COLS THEN
        cols = MIN_COLS
    ENDIF
    IF cols > MAX_COLS THEN
        cols = MAX_COLS
    ENDIF

    ' There must always be somewhere safe to click, so the original never
    ' let you have more mines than (height - 1) x (width - 1).
    DIM most AS INTEGER = (rows - 1) * (cols - 1)
    IF mines < MIN_MINES THEN
        mines = MIN_MINES
    ENDIF
    IF mines > most THEN
        mines = most
    ENDIF

    g.Dlg = DLG_NONE
    g.NewBoard(rows, cols, mines, LV_CUSTOM)
    g.SaveScores()          ' remember this board for next time
END SUB

' AcceptName files the high score the player has just typed a name for.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) AcceptName()
    DIM nm AS STRING = Trim(g.NameBuf)
    IF nm = "" THEN
        nm = "Anonymous"
    ENDIF
    DIM e AS ScoreEntry
    e.Secs = g.Secs
    e.Who = nm
    e.DateStr = time.Now().Format("2006-01-02")
    g.InsertScore(g.NameLvl, g.NameRank, e)
    g.Dlg = DLG_TIMES        ' then show the table, as the original did
END SUB

' UpdateDialog handles the mouse and keyboard while a dialog box is up.
'
' While a dialog is on screen nothing else in the game listens to you at
' all - clicking the minefield behind it does nothing. Programmers call
' that a MODAL dialog, and the way we get it is almost embarrassingly
' simple: Update calls this routine and then stops, so the code that would
' have handled the board never runs.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) UpdateDialog()
    DIM clicked AS BOOLEAN = inpututil.IsMouseButtonJustPressed(ebiten.MouseButtonLeft)
    DIM esc AS BOOLEAN = inpututil.IsKeyJustPressed(ebiten.KeyEscape)
    DIM enter AS BOOLEAN = inpututil.IsKeyJustPressed(ebiten.KeyEnter) OR inpututil.IsKeyJustPressed(ebiten.KeyNumpadEnter)

    ' Is the mouse on the rightmost button, or the one beside it?
    DIM hitB0 AS BOOLEAN = clicked AND HitRect(g.MX, g.MY, BtnX(g, 0), BtnY(g), BTNW, BTNH)
    DIM hitB1 AS BOOLEAN = clicked AND HitRect(g.MX, g.MY, BtnX(g, 1), BtnY(g), BTNW, BTNH)

    SELECT CASE g.Dlg
        CASE DLG_CUSTOM
            ' Tab steps from one box to the next.
            IF inpututil.IsKeyJustPressed(ebiten.KeyTab) THEN
                g.Focus = (g.Focus + 1) MOD 3
            ENDIF
            ' Clicking a box puts the cursor in it.
            IF clicked THEN
                DIM n AS INTEGER
                FOR n = 0 TO 2
                    IF HitRect(g.MX, g.MY, FldX(g), FldY(g, n), FLDW, FLDH) THEN
                        g.Focus = n
                    ENDIF
                NEXT
            ENDIF
            ' Anything typed goes into whichever box has the cursor.
            IF g.Focus = 0 THEN
                g.FldRows = TypedInto(g.FldRows, TRUE, 3)
            ELSEIF g.Focus = 1 THEN
                g.FldCols = TypedInto(g.FldCols, TRUE, 3)
            ELSE
                g.FldMines = TypedInto(g.FldMines, TRUE, 3)
            ENDIF

            IF enter OR hitB1 THEN
                g.ApplyCustom()
            ELSEIF esc OR hitB0 THEN
                g.Dlg = DLG_NONE
            ENDIF

        CASE DLG_NAME
            g.NameBuf = TypedInto(g.NameBuf, FALSE, 18)
            IF enter OR esc OR hitB0 THEN
                g.AcceptName()
            ENDIF

        CASE DLG_TIMES
            IF enter OR esc OR hitB0 THEN
                g.Dlg = DLG_NONE
            ENDIF

        CASE ELSE
            ' About and Help both just have an OK button.
            IF enter OR esc OR hitB0 THEN
                g.Dlg = DLG_NONE
            ENDIF
    END SELECT
END SUB

' UpdateMenu handles the mouse while a drop-down menu is open.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) UpdateMenu()
    IF inpututil.IsKeyJustPressed(ebiten.KeyEscape) THEN
        g.CloseMenu()
        RETURN
    ENDIF

    ' Highlight whatever line the mouse is resting on.
    DIM hot AS INTEGER = MenuHit(g, g.MX, g.MY)
    g.MenuHot = hot

    ' Sliding along the menu bar with a menu open switches to the other one,
    ' just like a real Windows menu bar.
    DIM t AS INTEGER = TitleHit(g.MX, g.MY)
    IF t >= 0 AND t <> g.MenuOpen THEN
        g.OpenMenu(t)
        RETURN
    ENDIF

    DIM press AS BOOLEAN = inpututil.IsMouseButtonJustPressed(ebiten.MouseButtonLeft)
    DIM release AS BOOLEAN = inpututil.IsMouseButtonJustReleased(ebiten.MouseButtonLeft)

    IF press OR release THEN
        IF hot >= 0 THEN
            ' Picking a line closes the menu and runs the command.
            DIM id AS INTEGER = g.MenuItems[hot].Id
            g.CloseMenu()
            g.DoCommand(id)
        ELSEIF press THEN
            IF t = g.MenuOpen THEN
                g.CloseMenu()          ' clicking the heading again closes it
            ELSE
                g.CloseMenu()          ' clicking anywhere else closes it too
            ENDIF
        ENDIF
    ENDIF
END SUB

' UpdateMouse is where the actual game is played: the menu bar, the smiley
' button and the minefield.
'
' The trickiest part is that the buttons do not do their work when they go
' DOWN, they do it when they come back UP - so you can press on a square,
' change your mind, slide off it and let go safely. That is how the original
' behaved and it is worth keeping.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) UpdateMouse()
    DIM over AS INTEGER = CellAt(g, g.MX, g.MY)
    DIM onFace AS BOOLEAN = HitRect(g.MX, g.MY, FaceX(g), FaceY(g), FACEW, FACEW)

    ' There are two different questions you can ask about a mouse button,
    ' and telling them apart is the whole secret of handling clicks:
    '   IsMouseButtonPressed      - is it down RIGHT NOW? (true for as long
    '                               as the player holds it, so sixty times a
    '                               second while they think about it)
    '   IsMouseButtonJustPressed  - did it go down THIS INSTANT? (true for
    '                               one single frame, then never again until
    '                               they let go and press it once more)
    ' Use the first for "is the player still holding this square down", and
    ' the second for "the player has just done something".
    DIM lDown AS BOOLEAN = ebiten.IsMouseButtonPressed(ebiten.MouseButtonLeft)
    DIM rDown AS BOOLEAN = ebiten.IsMouseButtonPressed(ebiten.MouseButtonRight)
    DIM mDown AS BOOLEAN = ebiten.IsMouseButtonPressed(ebiten.MouseButtonMiddle)

    DIM lPress AS BOOLEAN = inpututil.IsMouseButtonJustPressed(ebiten.MouseButtonLeft)
    DIM rPress AS BOOLEAN = inpututil.IsMouseButtonJustPressed(ebiten.MouseButtonRight)
    DIM mPress AS BOOLEAN = inpututil.IsMouseButtonJustPressed(ebiten.MouseButtonMiddle)

    DIM lUp AS BOOLEAN = inpututil.IsMouseButtonJustReleased(ebiten.MouseButtonLeft)
    DIM rUp AS BOOLEAN = inpututil.IsMouseButtonJustReleased(ebiten.MouseButtonRight)
    DIM mUp AS BOOLEAN = inpututil.IsMouseButtonJustReleased(ebiten.MouseButtonMiddle)

    ' --- Buttons going down ---
    IF lPress THEN
        IF g.MY < MENUH THEN
            DIM t AS INTEGER = TitleHit(g.MX, g.MY)
            IF t >= 0 THEN
                g.OpenMenu(t)
                RETURN
            ENDIF
        ELSEIF onFace THEN
            g.FaceDown = TRUE
        ELSEIF over >= 0 AND NOT g.Over THEN
            g.Pressing = TRUE
            g.PressIdx = over
            IF rDown THEN
                g.Chording = TRUE      ' right was already held: this is a chord
            ENDIF
        ENDIF
    ENDIF

    IF rPress AND over >= 0 AND NOT g.Over THEN
        IF lDown THEN
            g.Chording = TRUE          ' left was already held: this is a chord
            g.Pressing = TRUE
            g.PressIdx = over
        ELSE
            g.Mark(over)               ' a plain right click flags straight away
        ENDIF
    ENDIF

    IF mPress AND over >= 0 AND NOT g.Over THEN
        g.Chording = TRUE              ' the middle button chords on its own
        g.Pressing = TRUE
        g.PressIdx = over
    ENDIF

    ' --- Dragging: the square being held follows the mouse ---
    IF g.Pressing THEN
        IF lDown OR mDown OR (g.Chording AND rDown) THEN
            g.PressIdx = over          ' becomes -1 if dragged off the board
        ENDIF
    ENDIF

    ' --- Buttons coming back up ---
    IF lUp OR rUp OR mUp THEN
        IF g.FaceDown AND lUp THEN
            g.FaceDown = FALSE
            IF onFace THEN
                g.Restart()
            ENDIF
        ELSEIF g.Swallow THEN
            ' A chord needs two buttons, so the player has to let go of two
            ' buttons - but the chord should only happen once. The first
            ' release does the work and then sets Swallow, which means
            ' "ignore the next release, I have already dealt with it". We
            ' clear it again once the player's hand is completely off.
            IF NOT lDown AND NOT rDown AND NOT mDown THEN
                g.Swallow = FALSE
            ENDIF
        ELSEIF g.Chording THEN
            IF g.PressIdx >= 0 THEN
                g.Chord(g.PressIdx)
            ENDIF
            g.Chording = FALSE
            g.Pressing = FALSE
            g.PressIdx = -1
            ' If the other button is still held, ignore its release later.
            IF lDown OR rDown OR mDown THEN
                g.Swallow = TRUE
            ENDIF
        ELSEIF lUp AND g.Pressing THEN
            IF g.PressIdx >= 0 THEN
                g.Reveal(g.PressIdx)
            ENDIF
            g.Pressing = FALSE
            g.PressIdx = -1
        ENDIF
    ENDIF

    ' --- Which face is the smiley wearing? ---
    IF g.Over THEN
        IF g.Won THEN
            g.Face = FACE_WIN
        ELSE
            g.Face = FACE_DEAD
        ENDIF
    ELSEIF g.Pressing THEN
        g.Face = FACE_OOH
    ELSE
        g.Face = FACE_SMILE
    ENDIF
END SUB

' Update is one of the two routines Ebitengine calls sixty times a second.
' It looks at the mouse and keyboard and moves the game on; it never draws
' anything.
'
' Keeping "think" and "paint" in separate routines is a rule worth sticking
' to. It means there is exactly one place to look when the game does the
' wrong thing (here) and exactly one place to look when it looks wrong
' (Draw). Mix the two together and you will spend your evenings wondering
' why the score went up twice.
FUNCTION (g AS POINTER TO Game) Update() AS ERROR
    IF g.Quit THEN
        RETURN ebiten.Termination      ' this is how a game asks to close
    ENDIF

    DIM cx AS INTEGER
    DIM cy AS INTEGER
    cx, cy = ebiten.CursorPosition()
    g.MX = cx
    g.MY = cy
    g.Blink = g.Blink + 1
    g.UpdateView()

    ' --- The clock ---
    ' Update runs sixty times a second, so sixty of them is one second.
    IF g.Started AND NOT g.Over THEN
        g.Ticks = g.Ticks + 1
        IF g.Ticks >= 60 THEN
            g.Ticks = 0
            IF g.Secs < MAX_SECS THEN
                g.Secs = g.Secs + 1
                g.Play(g.SndTick)
            ENDIF
        ENDIF
    ENDIF

    ' A dialog box swallows everything while it is open.
    IF g.Dlg <> DLG_NONE THEN
        g.UpdateDialog()
        RETURN NIL
    ENDIF

    ' Keyboard shortcuts, the same two the original had.
    IF inpututil.IsKeyJustPressed(ebiten.KeyF2) THEN
        g.Restart()
    ENDIF
    IF inpututil.IsKeyJustPressed(ebiten.KeyF1) THEN
        g.Dlg = DLG_HELP
        RETURN NIL
    ENDIF

    IF g.MenuOpen >= 0 THEN
        g.UpdateMenu()
        RETURN NIL
    ENDIF

    g.UpdateMouse()
    RETURN NIL
END FUNCTION


' ======================== 8. DRAWING ========================

' Blit stamps one of our ready-made pictures onto the screen.
SUB Blit(dst AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image, img AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image, x AS INTEGER, y AS INTEGER)
    IF img = NIL THEN
        RETURN
    ENDIF
    DIM op AS ebiten.DrawImageOptions
    op.GeoM.Translate(Dbl(x), Dbl(y))
    dst.DrawImage(img, @op)
END SUB

' TextImg draws a piece of text into its own little image and remembers it.
' Asking for the same words again hands back the picture we made last time
' instead of drawing it all over again.
'
' THE SHADOW PROBLEM
'   The lettering we borrow is meant to be readable on top of ANYTHING - a
'   screenshot, a game world, whatever - so every letter comes with a black
'   shadow baked into it, sitting one pixel down and to the right.
'
'   That is a fine idea for debugging text floating over a picture. It is a
'   terrible idea here. We want plain black words on flat grey panels, and
'   we get our colours by tinting the whole letter as we stamp it down - so
'   the shadow gets tinted too, and instead of one crisp letter you see the
'   letter plus a smudged copy of itself half a pixel away. Small text turns
'   to mush.
'
'   So we rub the shadow out. Draw the words once, read the picture back a
'   pixel at a time, and make every dark pixel completely see-through. The
'   letters themselves are pure white, so telling the two apart is easy:
'   bright pixel, keep it; dark pixel, it is shadow, throw it away.
'
'   This only ever happens once per phrase, the first time it appears on
'   screen. After that the cleaned-up picture is reused.
FUNCTION TextImg(g AS POINTER TO Game, s AS STRING) AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image
    DIM cached AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image = GTxt[s]
    IF cached <> NIL THEN
        RETURN cached
    ENDIF

    DIM w AS INTEGER = Len(s) * CHW

    ' Draw the words once into a scratch picture. The lettering routine
    ' nudges everything one pixel to the right, so we ask for -1 to undo it.
    DIM scratch AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image = ebiten.NewImage(w, CHH)
    ebitenutil.DebugPrintAt(scratch, s, -1, 0)

    ' Fetch the picture back as a long list of numbers, four per pixel:
    ' how red it is, how green, how blue, and how solid ("alpha").
    DIM npix AS INTEGER = w * CHH * 4
    DIM buf AS BYTES = MakeBytes(npix)
    scratch.ReadPixels(buf)

    ' Walk the list four numbers at a time. If the pixel is dark it is part
    ' of the shadow, so set its solidity to nothing and it vanishes.
    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO npix - 4 STEP 4
        IF buf[i] < 128 THEN
            buf[i + 3] = 0
        ENDIF
    NEXT

    DIM img AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image = ebiten.NewImage(w, CHH)
    img.WritePixels(buf)
    GTxt[s] = img
    RETURN img
END FUNCTION

' DrawText paints some words on the screen in whatever colour we ask for.
'
' The lettering only comes in white, so to get black text - or red, or
' anything else - we tint the picture on its way to the screen, the way you
' would hold a coloured gel in front of a torch. (TextImg above has already
' scrubbed the shadow off, so what we are tinting is one clean letter.)
'
' Tinting multiplies each colour: white is "full brightness" and black is
' "no brightness", so white times black is black. And because only the
' brightness is touched, the see-through parts around each letter stay
' see-through, and the words sit neatly on whatever is behind them.
SUB DrawText(g AS POINTER TO Game, dst AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image, s AS STRING, x AS INTEGER, y AS INTEGER, clr AS color.RGBA)
    IF s = "" THEN
        RETURN
    ENDIF
    DIM img AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image = TextImg(g, s)
    DIM op AS ebiten.DrawImageOptions
    op.GeoM.Translate(Dbl(x), Dbl(y))
    op.ColorScale.ScaleWithColor(clr)
    dst.DrawImage(img, @op)
END SUB

' TextW is how many pixels wide a piece of text will be.
FUNCTION TextW(s AS STRING) AS INTEGER
    RETURN Len(s) * CHW
END FUNCTION

' --- The header: mine counter, smiley, clock ---

' DrawLed paints a three-digit red display showing the given number.
' Numbers below zero show a minus sign, which happens when you plant more
' flags than there are mines.
SUB DrawLed(g AS POINTER TO Game, dst AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image, value AS INTEGER, x AS INTEGER, y AS INTEGER)
    ' A thin black surround, then the three digits.
    Rect(dst, x - 1, y - 1, 3 * LEDW + 2, LEDH + 2, CBlack)

    DIM v AS INTEGER = value
    DIM neg AS BOOLEAN = FALSE
    IF v < 0 THEN
        neg = TRUE
        v = -v
    ENDIF
    IF neg AND v > 99 THEN
        v = 99
    ENDIF
    IF v > 999 THEN
        v = 999
    ENDIF

    ' Pull the three digits apart. For 142: 142/100 is 1 (whole numbers
    ' throw the remainder away), (142/10) MOD 10 is 14 with the 1 chopped
    ' off, giving 4, and 142 MOD 10 is the 2 left over at the end.
    DIM d0 AS INTEGER = v / 100
    DIM d1 AS INTEGER = (v / 10) MOD 10
    DIM d2 AS INTEGER = v MOD 10
    IF neg THEN
        d0 = 10                    ' picture number 10 is the minus sign
    ENDIF

    Blit(dst, g.ImgLed[d0], x, y)
    Blit(dst, g.ImgLed[d1], x + LEDW, y)
    Blit(dst, g.ImgLed[d2], x + 2 * LEDW, y)
END SUB

SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) DrawHeader(screen AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image)
    DIM innerW AS INTEGER = g.Cols * CELL
    Rect(screen, g.HdrX, g.HdrY, innerW, HDRH, CGray)

    DIM ledY AS INTEGER = g.HdrY + (HDRH - LEDH) / 2

    ' On the left, how many mines are left to find: the number buried,
    ' less the number of flags planted. It is only the player's own count,
    ' so it goes wrong if you flag the wrong squares - as it should.
    DrawLed(g, screen, g.NMines - g.NFlags, g.HdrX + 6, ledY)

    ' On the right, the clock.
    DrawLed(g, screen, g.Secs, g.HdrX + innerW - 6 - 3 * LEDW, ledY)

    ' In the middle, the smiley button.
    DIM fx AS INTEGER = FaceX(g)
    DIM fy AS INTEGER = FaceY(g)
    DIM onFace AS BOOLEAN = HitRect(g.MX, g.MY, fx, fy, FACEW, FACEW)
    IF g.FaceDown AND onFace THEN
        Blit(screen, g.ImgFaceDn, fx, fy)
    ELSE
        Blit(screen, g.ImgFace[g.Face], fx, fy)
    ENDIF
END SUB

' --- The minefield ---

' IsPressed says whether a square should be drawn pushed in. That is either
' the one square the player is holding down, or - during a chord - that
' square and all eight of its neighbours.
FUNCTION IsPressed(g AS POINTER TO Game, i AS INTEGER) AS BOOLEAN
    IF g.PressIdx < 0 OR g.Over THEN
        RETURN FALSE
    ENDIF
    IF NOT g.Chording THEN
        RETURN i = g.PressIdx
    ENDIF
    DIM r0 AS INTEGER = g.PressIdx / g.Cols
    DIM c0 AS INTEGER = g.PressIdx MOD g.Cols
    DIM r AS INTEGER = i / g.Cols
    DIM c AS INTEGER = i MOD g.Cols
    IF r < r0 - 1 OR r > r0 + 1 THEN
        RETURN FALSE
    ENDIF
    IF c < c0 - 1 OR c > c0 + 1 THEN
        RETURN FALSE
    ENDIF
    RETURN TRUE
END FUNCTION

SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) DrawField(screen AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image)
    DIM r AS INTEGER
    DIM c AS INTEGER
    FOR r = 0 TO g.Rows - 1
        FOR c = 0 TO g.Cols - 1
            DIM i AS INTEGER = r * g.Cols + c
            DIM px AS INTEGER = g.GX + c * CELL
            DIM py AS INTEGER = g.GY + r * CELL
            DIM img AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image = g.ImgCovered

            IF g.Over AND NOT g.Won THEN
                ' The game is lost, so everything is laid bare.
                IF g.Mine[i] THEN
                    IF i = g.BoomIdx THEN
                        img = g.ImgBoom          ' the one you stepped on
                    ELSEIF g.St[i] = ST_FLAG THEN
                        img = g.ImgFlag          ' you had this one right
                    ELSE
                        img = g.ImgMine
                    ENDIF
                ELSE
                    IF g.St[i] = ST_FLAG THEN
                        img = g.ImgWrong         ' a flag where there was no mine
                    ELSEIF g.St[i] = ST_REVEALED THEN
                        IF g.Adj[i] = 0 THEN
                            img = g.ImgBlank
                        ELSE
                            img = g.ImgNum[g.Adj[i] - 1]
                        ENDIF
                    ENDIF
                ENDIF
            ELSE
                SELECT CASE g.St[i]
                    CASE ST_FLAG
                        img = g.ImgFlag
                    CASE ST_QUERY
                        IF IsPressed(g, i) THEN
                            img = g.ImgQueryDn
                        ELSE
                            img = g.ImgQuery
                        ENDIF
                    CASE ST_REVEALED
                        IF g.Adj[i] = 0 THEN
                            img = g.ImgBlank
                        ELSE
                            img = g.ImgNum[g.Adj[i] - 1]
                        ENDIF
                    CASE ELSE
                        ' Covered. Held down means it is drawn already open.
                        IF IsPressed(g, i) THEN
                            img = g.ImgBlank
                        ENDIF
                END SELECT
            ENDIF

            Blit(screen, img, px, py)
        NEXT
    NEXT
END SUB

' --- The menu bar and its drop-downs ---

' DrawTick paints the small check mark shown beside a switched-on option.
SUB DrawTick(dst AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image, x AS INTEGER, y AS INTEGER, clr AS color.RGBA)
    Rect(dst, x, y + 3, 2, 3, clr)
    Rect(dst, x + 1, y + 5, 2, 2, clr)
    Rect(dst, x + 2, y + 4, 2, 2, clr)
    Rect(dst, x + 3, y + 2, 2, 2, clr)
    Rect(dst, x + 4, y + 1, 2, 2, clr)
    Rect(dst, x + 5, y, 2, 2, clr)
END SUB

SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) DrawMenuBar(screen AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image)
    Rect(screen, 0, 0, g.ViewW, MENUH, CGray)
    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO 1
        DIM label AS STRING = "Game"
        IF i = 1 THEN
            label = "Help"
        ENDIF
        DIM x AS INTEGER = TitleX(i)
        DIM w AS INTEGER = TitleW(label)
        DIM fg AS color.RGBA = CBlack
        IF g.MenuOpen = i THEN
            ' The heading of an open menu is highlighted.
            Rect(screen, x, 1, w, MENUH - 2, CNavy)
            fg = CWhite
        ENDIF
        DrawText(g, screen, label, x + 6, 2, fg)
        ' Underline the first letter, the way Windows marks the shortcut key.
        Rect(screen, x + 6, 15, CHW - 1, 1, fg)
    NEXT
END SUB

SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) DrawMenu(screen AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image)
    DIM x AS INTEGER = g.MenuX
    DIM y AS INTEGER = MENUH
    DIM w AS INTEGER = g.MenuW
    DIM h AS INTEGER = DropH(g)

    Rect(screen, x, y, w, h, CGray)
    Bevel(screen, x, y, w, h, 2, TRUE)

    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO Len(g.MenuItems) - 1
        DIM iy AS INTEGER = ItemY(g, i)
        IF g.MenuItems[i].Sep THEN
            ' A dividing line: one dark groove with a white highlight below.
            Rect(screen, x + 4, iy + 2, w - 8, 1, CDark)
            Rect(screen, x + 4, iy + 3, w - 8, 1, CWhite)
        ELSE
            DIM fg AS color.RGBA = CBlack
            IF i = g.MenuHot THEN
                Rect(screen, x + FRAME, iy, w - 2 * FRAME, ITEMH, CNavy)
                fg = CWhite
            ENDIF
            IF g.MenuItems[i].Checked THEN
                DrawTick(screen, x + 7, iy + 4, fg)
            ENDIF
            DrawText(g, screen, g.MenuItems[i].Label, x + LABELX, iy, fg)
            IF g.MenuItems[i].Accel <> "" THEN
                DrawText(g, screen, g.MenuItems[i].Accel, x + w - 10 - TextW(g.MenuItems[i].Accel), iy, fg)
            ENDIF
        ENDIF
    NEXT
END SUB

' --- Dialog boxes ---

' DrawButton paints one of the grey push buttons.
SUB DrawButton(g AS POINTER TO Game, dst AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image, x AS INTEGER, y AS INTEGER, w AS INTEGER, h AS INTEGER, label AS STRING)
    Rect(dst, x, y, w, h, CGray)
    ' A thin black outline, then the raised edge inside it.
    Rect(dst, x, y, w, 1, CBlack)
    Rect(dst, x, y + h - 1, w, 1, CBlack)
    Rect(dst, x, y, 1, h, CBlack)
    Rect(dst, x + w - 1, y, 1, h, CBlack)
    Bevel(dst, x + 1, y + 1, w - 2, h - 2, 2, TRUE)
    DrawText(g, dst, label, x + (w - TextW(label)) / 2, y + (h - CHH) / 2 + 1, CBlack)
END SUB

' DrawField3D paints one of the white boxes you type into.
SUB DrawField3D(g AS POINTER TO Game, dst AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image, x AS INTEGER, y AS INTEGER, w AS INTEGER, h AS INTEGER, text AS STRING, active AS BOOLEAN)
    Rect(dst, x, y, w, h, CWhite)
    Bevel(dst, x, y, w, h, 1, FALSE)
    DrawText(g, dst, text, x + 3, y + 1, CBlack)
    ' A blinking text cursor, but only in the box you are typing into.
    IF active AND (g.Blink MOD 60) < 30 THEN
        Rect(dst, x + 3 + TextW(text), y + 3, 1, h - 6, CBlack)
    ENDIF
END SUB

' DrawLines paints a stack of text lines one under the other.
SUB DrawLines(g AS POINTER TO Game, dst AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image, lines AS []STRING, x AS INTEGER, y AS INTEGER, lineH AS INTEGER)
    DIM i AS INTEGER
    FOR i = 0 TO Len(lines) - 1
        DrawText(g, dst, lines[i], x, y + i * lineH, CBlack)
    NEXT
END SUB

' DlgTitle is the wording along the top of each dialog.
FUNCTION DlgTitle(which AS INTEGER) AS STRING
    SELECT CASE which
        CASE DLG_CUSTOM
            RETURN "Custom Field"
        CASE DLG_TIMES
            RETURN "Fastest Bomb Squads"
        CASE DLG_NAME
            RETURN "Congratulations"
        CASE DLG_ABOUT
            RETURN "About BombSquad"
        CASE DLG_HELP
            RETURN "How To Play"
    END SELECT
    RETURN ""
END FUNCTION

SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) DrawDialog(screen AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image)
    DIM x AS INTEGER = DlgX(g)
    DIM y AS INTEGER = DlgY(g)
    DIM w AS INTEGER = DlgW(g)
    DIM h AS INTEGER = DlgHt(g)

    ' The panel: a black outline, a grey face and a raised edge.
    Rect(screen, x - 1, y - 1, w + 2, h + 2, CBlack)
    Rect(screen, x, y, w, h, CGray)
    Bevel(screen, x, y, w, h, 2, TRUE)

    ' The blue title bar.
    Rect(screen, x + 3, y + 3, w - 6, TITLEH, CNavy)
    DrawText(g, screen, DlgTitle(g.Dlg), x + 7, y + 4, CWhite)

    DIM top AS INTEGER = y + 3 + TITLEH

    SELECT CASE g.Dlg
        CASE DLG_CUSTOM
            DrawText(g, screen, "Height:", x + 14, FldY(g, 0) + 1, CBlack)
            DrawText(g, screen, "Width:", x + 14, FldY(g, 1) + 1, CBlack)
            DrawText(g, screen, "Mines:", x + 14, FldY(g, 2) + 1, CBlack)
            DrawField3D(g, screen, FldX(g), FldY(g, 0), FLDW, FLDH, g.FldRows, g.Focus = 0)
            DrawField3D(g, screen, FldX(g), FldY(g, 1), FLDW, FLDH, g.FldCols, g.Focus = 1)
            DrawField3D(g, screen, FldX(g), FldY(g, 2), FLDW, FLDH, g.FldMines, g.Focus = 2)
            DrawButton(g, screen, BtnX(g, 1), BtnY(g), BTNW, BTNH, "OK")
            DrawButton(g, screen, BtnX(g, 0), BtnY(g), BTNW, BTNH, "Cancel")

        CASE DLG_TIMES
            ' Three columns side by side, one per difficulty.
            DIM lvl AS INTEGER
            FOR lvl = LV_BEGINNER TO LV_EXPERT
                DIM cx AS INTEGER = x + 12 + lvl * SCORE_COLGAP
                DrawText(g, screen, LevelName(lvl), cx, top + 6, CNavy)
                Rect(screen, cx, top + 22, SCORE_COLW, 1, CDark)
                DIM list AS []ScoreEntry = g.ScoresFor(lvl)
                DIM n AS INTEGER
                FOR n = 0 TO TOP_N - 1
                    DIM ly AS INTEGER = top + 28 + n * 16
                    IF n < Len(list) THEN
                        DIM who AS STRING = list[n].Who
                        IF Len(who) > NAME_CHARS THEN
                            who = Left(who, NAME_CHARS)
                        ENDIF
                        DrawText(g, screen, Str(n + 1) & ". " & who, cx, ly, CBlack)
                        DIM secs AS STRING = Str(list[n].Secs)
                        DrawText(g, screen, secs, cx + SCORE_COLW - TextW(secs), ly, CBlack)
                    ELSE
                        DrawText(g, screen, Str(n + 1) & ". ---", cx, ly, CDark)
                    ENDIF
                NEXT
            NEXT
            DrawText(g, screen, "Times are in seconds. Custom boards are not recorded.", x + 12, top + 120, CDark)
            DrawButton(g, screen, BtnX(g, 0), BtnY(g), BTNW, BTNH, "OK")

        CASE DLG_NAME
            DIM msg AS []STRING
            APPEND(msg, "You have one of the fastest times")
            APPEND(msg, "for " & LevelName(g.NameLvl) & " level - " & Str(g.Secs) & " seconds.")
            APPEND(msg, "Please enter your name:")
            DrawLines(g, screen, msg, x + 14, top + 8, 15)
            DrawField3D(g, screen, x + 14, top + 58, w - 28, FLDH, g.NameBuf, TRUE)

            ' The table only has room for NAME_CHARS characters, so say so
            ' the moment a longer name is typed rather than quietly cutting
            ' it short later. The whole name is still saved to the file.
            IF NameTooLong(g.NameBuf) THEN
                DIM warnClr AS color.RGBA = CRed
                IF NOT g.UseColor THEN
                    warnClr = CBlack
                ENDIF
                DrawText(g, screen, NameWarning(g.NameBuf), x + 14, top + 80, warnClr)
            ENDIF

            DrawButton(g, screen, BtnX(g, 0), BtnY(g), BTNW, BTNH, "OK")

        CASE DLG_ABOUT
            DIM ab AS []STRING
            APPEND(ab, "BombSquad")
            APPEND(ab, "")
            APPEND(ab, "A minesweeping game in the style")
            APPEND(ab, "of the Windows classic, written in")
            APPEND(ab, "DBasic and drawn with Ebitengine.")
            APPEND(ab, "")
            APPEND(ab, "High scores are kept in:")
            ' A long home folder would run off the edge, so show the tail end.
            DIM where AS STRING = g.ScFile
            IF Len(where) > 40 THEN
                where = "..." & Right(where, 37)
            ENDIF
            APPEND(ab, where)
            DrawLines(g, screen, ab, x + 14, top + 8, 14)
            DrawButton(g, screen, BtnX(g, 0), BtnY(g), BTNW, BTNH, "OK")

        CASE ELSE
            DIM hp AS []STRING
            APPEND(hp, "The field hides mines. Uncover every square")
            APPEND(hp, "that is NOT a mine and you win.")
            APPEND(hp, "")
            APPEND(hp, "Left click ......... uncover a square")
            APPEND(hp, "Right click ........ flag a mine, then mark")
            APPEND(hp, "                     it '?', then clear it")
            APPEND(hp, "Both buttons ....... uncover the neighbours of")
            APPEND(hp, "  (or middle)        a number, once you have")
            APPEND(hp, "                     flagged that many of them")
            APPEND(hp, "Smiley face ........ start a new game")
            APPEND(hp, "F2 ................. start a new game")
            APPEND(hp, "")
            APPEND(hp, "A number says how many of the eight squares")
            APPEND(hp, "around it hold a mine. A blank square has")
            APPEND(hp, "none, so its neighbours open up for free.")
            APPEND(hp, "")
            APPEND(hp, "The left display counts the mines you have")
            APPEND(hp, "not flagged yet. The right one is a clock.")
            APPEND(hp, "Your first click is always safe.")
            DrawLines(g, screen, hp, x + 14, top + 8, 13)
            DrawButton(g, screen, BtnX(g, 0), BtnY(g), BTNW, BTNH, "OK")
    END SELECT
END SUB

' Draw is the other routine Ebitengine calls sixty times a second. It paints
' the whole window from scratch every time, and it never changes the state
' of the game - all the thinking happened in Update.
SUB (g AS POINTER TO Game) Draw(screen AS POINTER TO ebiten.Image)
    screen.Fill(CGray)

    g.DrawMenuBar(screen)

    ' The raised border around the whole playing area.
    Bevel(screen, g.OX, g.OY + MENUH, g.ScrW, g.ScrH - MENUH, OUTER, TRUE)

    ' The header panel sits in a sunken frame...
    Bevel(screen, g.HdrX - FRAME, g.HdrY - FRAME, g.Cols * CELL + 2 * FRAME, HDRH + 2 * FRAME, FRAME, FALSE)
    g.DrawHeader(screen)

    ' ...and so does the minefield.
    Bevel(screen, g.GX - FRAME, g.GY - FRAME, g.Cols * CELL + 2 * FRAME, g.Rows * CELL + 2 * FRAME, FRAME, FALSE)
    g.DrawField(screen)

    ' Menus and dialogs go on top of everything else.
    IF g.MenuOpen >= 0 THEN
        g.DrawMenu(screen)
    ENDIF
    IF g.Dlg <> DLG_NONE THEN
        g.DrawDialog(screen)
    ENDIF
END SUB

' Layout tells Ebitengine how big the game's picture is, in game pixels.
'
' This is a lovely arrangement. We draw a small picture - a Beginner board
' is only 168 pixels across, the same as the original game in 1990 - and
' Ebitengine blows it up to fill the window for us. So every measurement in
' this whole program is in original-sized pixels, and the Zoom setting costs
' us exactly nothing: no sums to double, nothing to remember. Change Zoom
' and the same picture simply arrives bigger.
FUNCTION (g AS POINTER TO Game) Layout(ow AS INTEGER, oh AS INTEGER) AS (INTEGER, INTEGER)
    RETURN g.ViewW, g.ViewH
END FUNCTION


' ======================== 9. MAIN ========================
'
' Every DBasic program starts here.

SUB Main()
    ' The random number generator seeds itself differently on every run, so
    ' the mines land somewhere new each time without us doing anything.

    ' Ebitengine allows exactly one audio context per program, so this is it.
    GAudio = audio.NewContext(RATE)

    DIM g AS Game

    ' --- Settings before anything is loaded from disk ---
    g.Zoom = 2                    ' everything drawn twice as big as the original
    g.UseMarks = TRUE
    g.UseColor = TRUE
    g.UseSound = TRUE
    g.MenuOpen = -1
    g.MenuHot = -1
    g.Dlg = DLG_NONE
    g.PressIdx = -1
    g.BoomIdx = -1
    g.Level = LV_BEGINNER

    ' The window is drawn at 1 pixel per original pixel and then blown up by
    ' Zoom. Pass a number on the command line to change that, for example
    ' "mines 3" for a really big board or "mines 1" for the original size.
    DIM args AS []STRING = os.Args
    IF Len(args) > 1 THEN
        DIM z AS INTEGER = Int(Val(args[1]))
        IF z >= 1 AND z <= 6 THEN
            g.Zoom = z
        ENDIF
    ENDIF

    ' Read the high scores, the menu options and the board we played last
    ' time. A first run simply finds nothing and keeps the defaults above.
    g.LoadScores()

    ' Draw all the artwork and generate all the sounds.
    g.MakeSprites()
    g.InitSounds()

    ' Deal the first board: whatever was being played last time, if it looked
    ' sensible, otherwise Beginner.
    IF g.Rows >= MIN_ROWS AND g.Rows <= MAX_ROWS AND g.Cols >= MIN_COLS AND g.Cols <= MAX_COLS AND g.NMines >= 1 AND g.NMines < g.Rows * g.Cols THEN
        g.NewBoard(g.Rows, g.Cols, g.NMines, g.Level)
    ELSE
        g.StartLevel(LV_BEGINNER)
    ENDIF

    ebiten.SetWindowTitle("BombSquad")
    ebiten.SetTPS(60)

    ' RunGame hands control over to Ebitengine, which from now on calls our
    ' Update, Draw and Layout routines until the window is closed.
    DIM err AS ERROR = ebiten.RunGame(@g)
    IF err <> NIL THEN
        fmt.Println("BombSquad stopped:", err)
    ENDIF
END SUB
